N2  - Background: Neoadjuvant anti-PD1 blockade improves surgical results in non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). The hypothesis of this study was that stereotactic radiation (SRT) could further enhance immune response to this therapy. Methods: This phase II trial included patients >18 years of age who had resectable, stage I-IIIA NSCLC. Patients who had chemotherapy before enrollment were eligible. Tumor tissue was collected at baseline. Two run-in cohorts received 2 cycles of pembrolizumab, 200 mg IV, q3 weeks (Arm A) or pembrolizumab followed by 12 Gy SRT to the lateral half of the primary tumor <7 days later (Arm B). A subsequent cohort was to be opened if both arms met prespecified safety endpoints, in which participants would be randomized between the 2 arms. Surgical resection occurred within 6 weeks after pembrolizumab cycle 2. The primary endpoint was the change in the number of infiltrating CD3+ T cells/ mm2 in primary tumor tissue evaluated by immunohistochemistry. This trial is registered with ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03217071. Results: Between September 2017 and January 2021, 13 patients were enrolled (6 on Arm A and 7 on Arm B). The study terminated after completion of the run-in cohorts due to the emergence of competing chemoimmunotherapy paradigms. Twelve patients (92.3%) started protocol-defined therapy. Eleven (91.7%) had resection, with 1 patient in Arm A declining surgery due to complete response (CR) on imaging. There was 1 case of lung torsion resulting in pneumonectomy in Arm A and 1 case of grade 4 immunerelated pneumonitis in Arm B. At a median follow-up of 60.2 (range 21.3 to 80.2) months, 4 patients (33.3%) developed distant metastases, but none recurred locoregionally. Rates of 1- and 5-year relapse-free and overall survival were 75.0% (95% CI: 54.1%-100.0%) and 66.7% (95% CI: 44.7%-99.5%), and 100.0% (95% CI: 100.0%-100.0%) and 68.7% (95% CI: 44.5%-100%), respectively. In Arm A, 2 patients (22.2%) had at least a major pathologic response in the primary tumor (1 with no viable cells, 1 with < 10% viable cells) and another (11.1%) achieved a pathologic CR in the lymph nodes. In Arm B, 1 patient (11.1%) who notably had 2 cycles of carboplatin/paclitaxel before enrollment had pathologic CR. Nine participants (75.0%) had pretreatment and post-treatment tissue for analysis. The median change in the number of infiltrating CD3+ T cells/mm2 was +577.5 (IQR: 3.1 to 1509.1) in Arm A and +817.7 (IQR: 344.0 to 1415.9) in Arm B (P=0.81). Three patients in each arm (60.0% in Arm A, 75.0% in Arm B; P=1.00) achieved > 2-fold increase in CD3+ T cell density. Conclusions: Neoadjuvant pembrolizumab enhanced tumor immune response, but 12 Gy SRT did not produce further enhanced or abscopal response. Safety and clinical outcomes were acceptable with high rates of locoregional control. Additional translational analyses continue, as alternative strategies combining (chemo)immunotherapy and SRT could be explored in this population.
