N2  - Background: Lung cancer, particularly non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), is a leading cause of cancer-related death. Surgical resection is the primary treatment for early-stage NSCLC. Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) and robotic-assisted thoracic surgery (RATS) are minimally invasive techniques used for lung resection, with RATS offering better precision but higher costs. This meta-analysis compares the perioperative outcomes of RATS and VATS in NSCLC patients. Methods: A systematic literature search was conducted in PubMed, Web of Science, NLM, CNKI, and Wanfang for RCTs published between July 31, 2021, and December 31, 2024. Primary outcomes included operation time, blood loss, lymph node dissection, conversion to thoracotomy, chest drainage time, and hospital stay. Statistical analyses were performed using RevMan 5.3, with heterogeneity assessed using the I² statistic. A p-value of less than 0.05 was deemed significant. Results: Three RCTs were analyzed. Blood loss showed no significant difference (MD: -33.00; 95% CI: -85.51 to 22.89; p = 0.07). Conversion to thoracotomy was comparable (MD: 0.69; 95% CI: 0.37 to 1.28; p = 0.24). Hospital stay did not differ significantly (MD: 0.13; 95% CI: -0.58 to 0.84; p = 0.74). Operation time remained similar (MD: -4.63; 95% CI: -10.93 to 1.67; p = 0.15). VATS enabled significantly greater lymph node dissection (MD: 1.83; 95% CI: 0.16 to 3.49; p = 0.03). Chest drainage duration showed no difference (MD: 0.00; 95% CI: -0.27 to 0.27; p = 1.00). Conclusion: RATS and VATS yield comparable outcomes in NSCLC, with RATS potentially reducing blood loss, while VATS allows more lymph node dissection. Further high-quality RCTs are needed to clarify the optimal approach for NSCLC.
