N2  - INTRODUCTION: Lung cancer remains the leading cause of cancer-related mortality, making timely intervention crucial; this is evident in cases of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), which constitutes 85% of all new lung cancer diagnoses and where early surgical management is critical. Traditional management protocols involving staged biopsy and resection can lead to delays in treatment and the potential for disease progression. Streamlining this approach using single anesthesia single anesthesia event robotic bronchoscopy and thoracic surgery has the potential to expedite precise, lung-sparing, definitive surgical treatment. In this ongoing retrospective case series, we highlight our experience with this approach in managing early-stage NSCLC. METHODS: We reviewed the clinical records of patients with suspicious lung lesions who underwent a single-anesthesia event combining robotic navigational bronchoscopy (Ion™ robotic platform) and robotic-assisted thoracic surgery. These patients were selected following multidisciplinary assessment. The collected data included patient demographics, nodule features (size, location, PET avidity), procedure duration, intraoperative pathology results, and postoperative outcomes (chest tube duration, length of stay, and any complications). We used descriptive statistics to assess feasibility, safety, and procedural efficiency. RESULTS: We identified seven cases with an average nodule size of 14.25 mm (SD = 7.62). The majority of lesions were located in the left upper lobe (57%, n=4). The majority (57%, n=4) of cases were performed for peripheral nodule marking using ICG, followed by wedge resection. Three cases were performed for diagnosis followed by resection if positive; in two patients (29%) an intraprocedural diagnosis of lung adenocarcinoma led to immediate lobectomy, and in one patient, resection was not performed as robotic bronchoscopic biopsy did not confirm malignancy. The average combined procedural duration was 246 minutes, and the average hospital stay was 1.7 days. No major complications were noted; only one patient experienced an intermittent air leak that resolved without intervention. All resected cases achieved negative margin status. CONCLUSION: This case series exhibits the feasibility and safety of a single-anesthetic approach combining robotic bronchoscopy and thoracic surgery for diagnosing and managing suspicious lung nodules. Further studies with larger cohorts are needed to confirm these findings and assess long-term outcomes.
