N2  - Introduction: Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related mortality in the Philippines and worldwide. Endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) has emerged the procedure of choice in diagnosing lung and mediastinal masses, with its minimally invasive approach in sampling mediastinal structures, peripheral nodules and endobronchial lesions smaller than 2cm. This negates the need for an invasive surgical approach, decreasing operative complications and hospital costs. At early stages, lung resection surgery is first-line; however, for those unable to tolerate due to poor lung reserve or other medical conditions, stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) as part of the standard of care, is becoming increasingly accessible. Case description: A 68 year old female who previously underwent curative surgery for Stage 1 lung adenocarcinoma came in due to possible tumor recurrence. Four years into her disease surveillance, Chest CT and PET scan revealed a right hilar soft tissue density with 0.7 x 0.5 cm size adjacent to the metallic clips of the previous lobectomy site. After 1 year, it increased to 1.1 x 1.2 cm and became FDG-avid, hence tumor recurrence was considered. Due to the technical difficulty of accessing the lesion percutaneously, its small size, hilar location and proximity to vessels, she was referred to our institution for EBUS-guided biopsy. Bronchoscopy showed intact right lower lobectomy stump with mucosal irregularities and grossly irregular surgical staples (Figure 1). Linear EBUS of the right hilar lesion showed heterogeneous echogenicity, and transbronchial needle aspiration biopsy was positive for non-small cell carcinoma. With poor calculated lung function post-surgery, she was referred to radiation oncology and received a total dose of 6,000 cGy in fifteen (15) fractions to the right hilar mass as curative treatment. All procedures were successfully done as outpatient, obviating risk of an expensive, morbid open thoracic surgery. Discussion: Staple-line thickening adjacent to postsurgical sites in NSCLC is frequent, but tumor recurrence is rare. This report highlights the value of EBUS not just in early detection of cancer, but also its recurrence in rare sites inaccessible by conventional bronchoscopic modalities. With limitations of age and comorbidities, local recurrences can be treated with SBRT over salvage surgery, which offers equivalent local disease control.
