N2  - Introduction: Bronchoscopic lung volume reduction (BLVR) is generally contraindicated in patients with active malignancy. We present a case of a patient with severe emphysema who underwent BLVR and was subsequently diagnosed with squamous cell carcinoma (SCC), ultimately requiring management with photodynamic therapy (PDT) without removal of the valves. Case: A 68-year-old male with severe emphysema and a 25-pack-year smoking history underwent placement of four endobronchial valves in the left lower lobe. During the procedure, an unexpected superficial abnormality was identified in the bronchus intermedius, which could not be seen on prior imaging. Later, biopsy confirmed invasive SCC. Invasive mediastinal staging using endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) revealed no signs of lymphangitic spread. PET and EBUS staging confirmed that the malignancy was confined to the endobronchial region. Given the patient's poor surgical candidacy in the setting of stage I SCC, lung resection was not offered. However, the patient experienced a remarkable improvement in quality of life immediately post BLVR, and the decision was made to keep the valves in place during treatment. Local treatment with endoscopic ablation and multiple rounds of PDT was initiated for the primary SCC. Post bronchial biopsies thus far have been negative for malignancy. Discussion: The gold standard for stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is surgical resection. If he was a candidate, the valves would have been removed prior to surgery. As this was not an option and he had such a profound symptomatic improvement, we decided to proceed with staging and treatment of his lung cancer without removal of the valves. The valves did not lose effectiveness despite multiple bronchoscopies that could have dislodged the valves. Conclusion: This case illustrates that, with a multidisciplinary approach, BLVR can be safely maintained in select patients with emphysema even after the discovery of concurrent early-stage malignancy. Despite concerns regarding cancer diagnosis post-BLVR, effective local cancer control was achieved through ablation and PDT. This case underscores the crucial role of individualized decision-making in complex patients, empowering clinicians to consider concurrent management of emphysema and early-stage lung cancer.
