N2  - Rationale: Stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) and sub-lobar resection are the main treatment options for patients with early-stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) that are deemed too high-risk for lobectomy. Disparities in surgical management of patients with early-stage NSCLC have been demonstrated by multiple studies. In this study we sought to evaluate if physicians' therapeutic plans for early-stage NSCLC patients at high risk for lobectomy varied according to patient race and ethnicity. Methods: Subjects with clinical stage I-IIA NSCLC who were considered for sub-lobar resection or SBRT were recruited from five US healthcare systems. The main lung cancer treating provider for each patient was interviewed pre-treatment and asked to rate the patient's probability that 1) they were an ideal candidate for sub-lobar resection; and 2) the provider would recommend sublobar resection. We used generalized linear mixed models (with random provider intercept) to assess the relationship between patient's race and ethnicity with the provider's reported likelihood that the patient was an ideal candidate or that the provider would recommend sub-lobar resection. We adjusted for baseline differences in patient characteristics according to treatment using inverse probability weights. Results: A total of 337 patients (63% treated with SBRT) and 33 providers were enrolled. In adjusted analysis, Black race was not associated with lower odds of being rated a moderately (odds ratio [OR]: 1.60, 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.48 to 5.28) or highly ideal (OR: 0.58, 95% CI: 0.09 to 3.61) candidate for sub-lobar resection compared to white patients. Hispanic ethnicity was a predictor of greater odds of moderately ideal (OR: 5.95, 95% CI: 1.54 to 23.05) but not of highly ideal (OR: 3.8, 95% CI: 0.66 to 21.85) candidacy for sub-lobar resection. Black race was not associated with either moderate (OR: 0.54, 95% CI: 0.14 to 2.02) or high (OR: 0.58, 95% CI: 0.10 to 3.15) likelihood of being recommended for sublobar resection, nor was there an association between Hispanic ethnicity and moderate (OR: 4.55, 95% CI: 0.79 to 26.04) or high (OR: 3.45, 95% CI: 0.52 to 22.92) likelihood of being recommended for sub-lobar resection. Conclusions: We found that patient race or ethnicity were not associated with physician assessment of ideal candidacy for sub-lobar resection, nor did it predict which patients would be recommended surgery. This data suggests that system- and patient-level factors may play a larger role than provider bias in surgical inequities in early-stage lung cancer.
