N2  - Introduction: Minimally invasive surgery (MIS) for lung cancer resection are associated with improved outcomes. We performed a nationwide analysis examining trends in the use of different approaches for lung cancer resection. Methods: Trends in approach (VATS, robotic, or open) for patients that underwent lobectomy for stage IA non-small cell lung cancer from 2010-2020 in the NCDB were examined. To characterize pattern changes, facilities were classified as open, VATS, or robotic if ≥50% of cases were performed via that approach in the early period of the study (2010-2011), with facilities where all approaches were <50% categorized as mixed. Results: Overall, 121603 patients who had lobectomy at 1007 facilities met criteria. Open was the most common approach in 2010 (72% of cases), but both robotic (45%) and VATS (30%) approaches were more often used by 2020. VATS use trended upward from 2010-2017 but then plateaued, while robotic use increased from 2016 onward. Open remained the most common approach at open facilities until 2019, after which robotic approaches were used most often. The use of robotic approaches increased at VATS facilities such that VATS and robotic approach use were similar by 2020, but robotic facilities remained predominantly robotic through the study period. Conclusion: The use of MIS for lung cancer resection increased from 2010-2020. This change appears mostly due to open facilities converting to robotic approach, but VATS facilities also more commonly used robotic approach. This data suggests that the robotic approach facilitates transition to MIS.
