N2  - SESSION TITLE: Cancer Therapeautics SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Immunotherapy has become a mainstay treatment for several cancer types, and research continues to evaluate its efficacy in different histologies and treatment settings. Pseudoprogression (PP) with immunotherapy use is a relatively rare and poorly understood phenomenon. We discuss a case of immunotherapy-related PP in a patient with lung adenocarcinoma undergoing neoadjuvant therapy prior to curative surgery. CASE PRESENTATION: A 79-year-old former heavy smoker female with history of hypertension and pseudogout presented to the hospital with concerns for chest pain. Chest CT revealed a 5.1 cm RUL spiculated mass and a 1.5 cm nodule in the left breast. PET scan showed FDG-avidity in the RUL nodule, right hilar lymph nodes, and a nodule in the left breast without distant metastatic disease. Left breast biopsy showed an invasive carcinoma. The patient underwent EBUS bronchoscopy, which showed KRAS G12C mutant lung adenocarcinoma with negative PD-L1 expression. After staging as IIIB (cT3pN2cM0), she received only two of three cycles of neoadjuvant carboplatin, pemetrexed, and nivolumab due to side effects. Within a week of completing cycle 2, she was evaluated for hypotension with a chest CTA demonstrating new pleural nodules, concerning for disease progression. Pleuroscopy was performed, and biopsies of the pleural lesions were negative for metastatic disease, showing instead chronic inflammatory changes and reactive vascular proliferation. The patient was taken for VATS with RUL lobectomy and concomitant left magseed breast lumpectomy. The patient opted for surveillance with 6-month scans rather than adjuvant immunotherapy. She was started on endocrine therapy for stage I breast cancer after surgery. DISCUSSION: Pseudoprogression (PP) is described as an initial increase in tumor burden, both size and/or development of new lesions, followed by tumor regression after immunotherapy treatment [1]. While the mechanism of PP is incompletely understood, studies thus far have shown involvement of immune cells, necrosis, hemorrhage, and edema [1]. This phenomenon was initially described with the use of ipilimumab in metastatic melanoma; however, it has been seen in nearly all cancer types treated with each class of immunotherapy [1,2]. One study estimates the rate of PP at about 8.6%, with incidence of PP in NSCLC being about 2.8%-8.8% [1,3]. Several modalities can be utilized to assess for PP, including biopsy, biomarkers (cell-free DNA), and, much less reliably, imaging such as MRI and PET scans [4]. Oftentimes when PP is mistaken for true disease progression (TP), there is cessation of treatment. To help physicians better differentiate between PP and TP, irRC and irRECIST have been created to help assess progression in those treated with immunotherapy [5]. One small study found that RECIST v1.1 determined two patients to have TP while irRC did not; these patients ultimately had tumor regression [6]. Currently, there is data to suggest that those with PP have increased PFS and OS, though further investigational studies are necessary [7,8]. CONCLUSIONS: The physician caring for patients receiving these therapies must be aware of pseudoprogression to avoid the risk of discontinuing treatments prematurely, especially in the context of neoadjuvant therapies in patients treated with curative intent. This case underscores the need for histological assessment and confirmation of such instances. REFERENCE #1: 1. Jia W, Gao Q, Han A, Zhu H, Yu J. The potential mechanism, recognition and clinical significance of tumor pseudoprogression after immunotherapy. Cancer Biol Med. 2019;16(4):655-670. doi:10.20892/j.issn.2095-3941.2019.0144. 2. Di Giacomo AM, Danielli R, Guidoboni M, et al. Therapeutic efficacy of ipilimumab, an anti-CTLA-4 monoclonal antibody, in patients with metastatic melanoma unresponsive to prior systemic treatments: clinical and immunological evidence from three patient cases. Cancer Immunol Immunother. 2009;58(8):1297-1306. doi:10.1007/s00262-008-0642-y. 3. McKendry K, Desmond F, Ward C, Duffy AG. Pseudoprogression and cancer immunotherapy: A seven year retrospective study of rate, temporal course, and predictive markers in an Irish tertiary referral center. Journal of Clinical Oncology. 2021;39(15_suppl):2651-2651. doi:10.1200/JCO.2021.39.15_suppl.2651 REFERENCE #2: 4. Ma Y, Wang Q, Dong Q, Zhan L, Zhang J. How to differentiate pseudoprogression from true progression in cancer patients treated with immunotherapy. Am J Cancer Res. 2019;9(8):1546-1553. 5. Somarouthu B, Lee SI, Urban T, Sadow CA, Harris GJ, Kambadakone A. Immune-related tumour response assessment criteria: a comprehensive review. Br J Radiol. 2018;91(1084):20170457. doi:10.1259/bjr.20170457. 6. Kim HK, Heo MH, Lee HS, et al. Comparison of RECIST to immune-related response criteria in patients with non-small cell lung cancer treated with immune-checkpoint inhibitors. Cancer Chemother Pharmacol. 2017;80(3):591-598. doi:10.1007/s00280-017-3396-4 REFERENCE #3: 7. Chiou VL, Burotto M. Pseudoprogression and Immune-Related Response in Solid Tumors. Journal of Clinical Oncology. 2015;33(31):3541-3543. doi:10.1200/JCO.2015.61.6870. 8. Won SE, Park HJ, Byun S, et al. Impact of pseudoprogression and treatment beyond progression on outcome in patients with non-small cell lung cancer treated with immune checkpoint inhibitors. Oncoimmunology. 2020;9(1):1776058. doi:10.1080/2162402X.2020.1776058 DISCLOSURES: Advisory Committee Member relationship with Bristol Myers Squibb Please note: 2022-2024 Added 04/04/2024 by Debora Bruno, source=Web Response, value=Travel Advisory Committee Member relationship with AstraZeneca Please note: 2020-2024 Added 04/04/2024 by Debora Bruno, source=Web Response, value=Grant Research Funds Advisory Committee Member relationship with Merck Please note: 2023-2024 Added 04/04/2024 by Debora Bruno, source=Web Response, value=Honoraria Advisory Committee Member relationship with Mirati Therapeutics Please note: 2022-2024 Added 04/04/2024 by Debora Bruno, source=Web Response, value=Travel Advisory Committee Member relationship with Regeneron Please note: 2023-2024 Added 04/04/2024 by Debora Bruno, source=Web Response, value=Travel Advisory Committee Member relationship with Amgen Please note: 2022-2023 Added 04/04/2024 by Debora Bruno, source=Web Response, value=Honoraria No relevant relationships by Gregory Guzik
