N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Cancer Abstracts Posters (C) SESSION TYPE: Original Investigation Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/08/2024 01:45 pm - 02:30 pm PURPOSE: Lung cancer remains a leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide, but long-term survival is seen with curative resection. A recent NEJM article found that sublobar resection was not inferior to lobectomy for disease-free survival (Altorki et al. Lobar or Sublobar Resection for Peripheral Stage IA Non-Small-Cell Lung Cancer. N Engl J Med. 2023 Feb 9;388(6):489-498). We examined the successful implementation of ICG coil dye marking with robotic bronchoscopy followed by lung-sparring robotic resections. METHODS: 17 procedures (19 targets) were performed between March 2023 and May 2024. Successful lesion localization and coil placement was 94.7% (18 of 19 targets). Targeted lobes were 4 right upper, 6 right lower, 4 left upper, 3 left lower. Lesions were 6-22 mm in size (mean 10 mm solid component). Surgery occurred within an average of 2 days (0-6) after coil placement. Dye was visualized intraoperatively in 17 of 18 (94.4%) targets. RESULTS: Thirteen patients had robotic wedge resections, four had robotic segmentectomies. NSCLC was resected in 13 patients. One resection was unable to be performed due to adhesions and patient was referred to radiation oncology. Two patients had benign disease (fibrosis and hamartoma). CONCLUSIONS: Coil dye marking is a feasible and safe modality to facilitate minimally invasive surgical resection of early stage lung cancer. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Robotic bronchoscopy coil dye marking for early stage peripheral lung cancer is a helpful modality to assist in lung sparing robotic surgical resection. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Christina Bellinger No relevant relationships by Alberto de Hoyos No relevant relationships by Sable Skewes
