N2  - Purpose: For early-stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), surgery is the preferred approach in operable patients, whereas stereotactic ablative radiotherapy (SABR) is preferred for medically inoperable patients. The combination of neoadjuvant SABR followed by surgery was explored in the MISSILE phase II trial and was hypothesized to improve outcomes by cytoreducing tumours before surgery and/or inducing antitumour immune activity. Herein, we report long-term clinical outcomes, beyond five years of follow-up. Materials and Methods: Patients diagnosed with T1-2N0M0 NSCLC with ECOG 0-2 and postoperative predicted FEV1 ≥ 30% were enrolled. Patients underwent neoadjuvant SABR using a risk-adapted fractionation schema (with all dose fractionations exceeding a biological effective dose of at least 100Gy10) followed by lobectomy/wedge resection. The primary endpoint was pathological complete response (pCR). Other endpoints included major pathologic response (MPR), overall survival (OS), progression-free survival (PFS), cancer-specific survival (CSS), local control (LC), regional control (RC), distant control (DC), toxicity (based on the Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events [CTCAE]) and quality of life (QOL) (based on the Functional Assessment of Cancer Therapy: General [FACT-G]). Results: Forty patients received SABR, and 90% of these (n=36) proceeded with surgery. The pCR rate, as previously reported, was 60% and the MPR rate was 63%. For patients who received SABR and surgery, median follow-up was 6.6 years. Five-year OS was 66.7% (95% CI: 48.8-79.5), PFS was 58.3% (95% CI: 40.7-72.4) and CSS was 76.4% (95% CI: 58.2-87.4). Five-year LC, RC and DC were 93.5% (95% CI: 76.3-98.4), 80.1% (95% CI: 62.7-90.0) and 82.4% (95% CI: 64.9-91.7) respectively. After SABR and surgery, 13.9% (n=5) of patients experienced related Grade ≥3 adverse events. Observed Grade 4 adverse events were atrial fibrillation, bronchopleural fistula and respiratory failure. There were no Grade 5 adverse events. FACT-G total showed significant declines in the post-surgery period that were worst at 12-months post-surgery (mean ± SD: 84.7 ± 16.1 versus 91.5 ± 12.2, p=0.009) but recovered thereafter. Predictors of improved OS on univariable analysis were T1 versus T2 tumour size (hazard ratio [HR]: 0.33, 95% CI: 0.13-0.85, p=0.021), increasing post-treatment FEV1(HR per 10%: 0.59, 95% CI: 0.37-0.95, p=0.028), and VATS versus open surgical approach (HR: 0.19, 95% CI: 0.04-0.90, p=0.037). Conclusions: A combined approach of SABR and surgery achieved results that are similar to the use of either modality alone. A combined approach may be considered in situations where surgery is desired but operating room availability is restricted, or if temporary medical factors preclude immediate surgery.
