N2  - Background: In March 2022, based on outcomes from the CheckMate-816 trial, the FDA approved nivolumab in combination with platinum doublet chemotherapy (PDC) for the neoadjuvant treatment of adult patients with resectable (tumors ≥4 cm or node positive) non-small cell lung cancer (rNSCLC). The purpose of this research is to estimate the cost of adverse events (AE), surgery, and medical resource use (MRU) over the first year of treatment in patients receiving neoadjuvant nivolumab plus PDC (N+PDC) versus neoadjuvant PDC (neoPDC) for rNSCLC from a US commercial payer perspective. Methods: The frequency of AE, surgery, and MRU were combined with unit costs from US published databases to estimate the cost per average patient treated with N+PDC versus neoPDC. Data on AE (all grades and those related to surgical complications), surgery type and approach, and treatment patterns were sourced from CheckMate-816. Due to a lack of published MRU data specific to the neoadjuvant setting in the US, MRU was sourced from retrospective observational studies of US patients with complete resection of early-stage NSCLC. Surgery costs were derived from longitudinal studies that assessed the cost of surgical resection for lung cancer in the US. Results: The estimated cost of AE, surgery, and MRU in the first year of treatment was estimated to be 5.7% lower per patient receiving N+PDC compared to neoPDC ($49,361 vs. $52,176), primarily driven by lower costs associated with neoadjuvant AE ($5,380 vs. $7,223), adjuvant AE ($220 vs. $2,309), and surgical complications ($381 vs. $1,584). Patients receiving N+PDC were estimated to incur fewer adjuvant AE costs due to reduced use of adjuvant therapy (14.5% vs. 24.6%) and a less costly mix of adjuvant AE. The estimated cost of surgery per average patient receiving N+PDC was slightly higher than neoPDC ($32,956 vs. $30,785) as more patients undergo definitive surgery (83.2% vs. 75.4%). The estimated cost of surgery for patients receiving surgery was slightly lower for patients receiving N+PDC compared to neoPDC ($39,610 vs. $40,828) driven by greater eligibility for minimally invasive (29.5% vs. 21.5%) and less costly surgeries such as lobectomy (77.2% vs. 60.7%) versus pneumonectomy (16.8% vs. 25.5%). Estimated MRU costs were slightly higher for patients receiving N+PDC ($5,929 vs. $5,065) due to pre-medications prescribed for the prevention of immune-mediated AE. Conclusions: Patients receiving N+PDC incurred lower estimated costs from AE, surgery, and other MRU from the US commercial payer perspective over the first year of treatment compared to neoPDC.
