N2  - Purpose Pulsed Electric Fields (PEF) does not rely on thermal mechanisms to induce cell injury and demise. This may result in several therapeutic advantages including the potential to treat lesions nearer to sensitive structures, the potential to drive a host immune response in the setting of cancer, etc. The VIGOR protocol aims to evaluate the impact of PEF energy in a neo-adjuvant NSCLC setting when combined with the CheckMate (CM) 816 protocol: 3 cycles of nivolumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy, followed by surgical resection. Methods This prospective study evaluates adults with a suspected or confirmed stage IIB-IIIA NSCLC deemed suitable candidates to receive the CM 816 protocol followed by surgical resection. Following diagnostic biopsy and staging, subjects then received PEF energy (AliyaTM System, Galvanize Therapeutics, Redwood City, CA) to the primary lesion. This was followed by the CM 816 protocol within 14 days of energy delivery. Surgical resection occurred within 6 weeks of the last infusion. Blood and tissue samples were collected for pre- and post-PEF comparison for genomic, immunologic, pathologic, and biomarker assessments. Safety and efficacy endpoints were evaluated, including assessment of PEF device and/or procedure-related serious adverse events, changes in surgical approach, and pathologic assessment of the entirety of the resected tissue. Results PEF was successfully delivered in five subjects, (median age of 65 yrs; primary lesion median 1.8 cm). No PEF-related serious adverse events were observed. All five subjects commenced neoadjuvant therapy. Of those 5 subjects, 3 completed the therapy prior to resection. One subject is currently receiving the CM 816 regimen. In one subject, the CM 816 regimen was discontinued subsequent to an additional diagnosis of pancreatic cancer and did not undergo resection. PEF delivery combined with the CM 816 protocol resulted in 2 complete pathologic responses and the potential of 1 major pathologic response on the preliminary pathologic analysis in the three subjects who underwent thoracoscopic lobectomy. Conclusions The neoadjuvant delivery of PEF followed by the CM 816 protocol does not appear to impact the ability to tolerate either the systemic therapy or the planned surgical resections. At present, the addition of PEF to the CM 816 does not appear to adversely affect the surgical field or the ability to assess pathologic response.
