N2  - Background: Post-operative pain is a significant concern in thoracic surgery. However, there is limited data comparing post-operative pain levels following robotic thoracic surgery and videoassisted thoracic surgery for lung cancer. This study aims to evaluate and compare the postoperative pain experiences of patients undergoing these two surgical approaches. Methods: A retrospective analysis was performed on 205 patients from the electronic medical records of Stonybrook Medical Center. Among them, 84 patients underwent robotic thoracic surgery, while 121 patients underwent video-assisted thoracic surgery with lobectomy for stage 1 non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). Pain scales were reviewed at the day of surgery, day of discharge, and 2- week post-operative visits, utilizing the standard numerical scale ranging from 0 to 10. Mann Whitney test was used to compare the reported pain levels between the two procedures. Results: A total 205 adult, thoracic surgery patients were reviewed. 84 underwent Robotic surgery and 121 video-assisted thoracic surgery. Age at presentation was similar between both groups (95% CL: 63-82). The mean pain levels for day of surgery, date of discharge (day 3-5), 2 weeks postoperative with a 95% confidence interval on a 0-10 scare were calculated. The respective time points for video-assisted vs robotic surgery were as follows: Day 1 (6 vs 8), discharge (5 vs 6), and 2 weeks (5 vs 2). Conclusions: Robotic thoracic surgery demonstrated lower reported pain levels compared to video-assisted thoracic surgery, with the most significant difference being observed at the 2-week post-operative visit. Further prospective studies involving larger patient cohorts are warranted.
