N2  - Rationale:Patients diagnosed with biopsy-proven stage IA non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) have a range of treatment options for primary therapy. The patient can undergo surgical resection, if deemed an appropriate candidate, which serves as the current standard for treatment. However, in patients who do not meet surgical criteria, or decline surgery, stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) or image-guided thermal ablation (IGTA) may serve as primary therapy. Microwave ablation (MWA), a form of IGTA, has been shown to be safe and efficacious for the treatment of Stage IA NSCLC. We present a comparison of MWA, SBRT, and resection as primary therapy for early-stage NSCLC in a US veteran population. Methods:Retrospective, singleinstitution, review of pulmonary nodules from 6/2016 to 9/2023 was conducted, identifying 490 patients. Only patients undergoing MWA, SBRT, or resection of Stage IA NSCLC were included in the study. MWA was performed by a single operator using 2.45 GHz, gas-cooled system (NeuWave/Ethicon, Madison, WI). Resection composed of either lobectomy, sublobar/wedge resection, or pneumonectomy. Lesion characteristics, patient demographics, and adverse events were examined. Local tumor progression free survival (LT-PFS), NSCLC progression free survival (PFS), and overall survival (OS) were analyzed with Kaplan-Meier, Cox regression and log rank test (SAS, Cary, NC). Results:Treatment of stage IA NSCLC occurred in 178 patients (MWA n=60, SBRT n=30, Resection n=88). For MWA, technical success was 100% with one prolonged air-leak that resolved at 20 days. All cause 90-day mortality for the MWA group was 1.6% n=1. Patients in the resection group underwent lobectomy n=50, segmentectomy n=37, or pneumonectomy n=1. All cause 90-day mortality for the resection group was 6.81%, n=6. Chest tube placement accompanied the MWA n=22 (36.6%) and resection n=88 (100%). If admitted, the average total length of ICU stay was longer for the resection group compared to the MWA group (4.1 vs 1.0 days, p<0.001). All cause 90-day mortality for the SBRT group was 3.33% n=1. Estimated LT-PFS, PFS, and OS can be found in the attached table for MWA, resection, and SBRT. For LT-PFS, PFS, and OS, no statistically significant difference was found between the three treatment groups when compared individually (log rank p>0.05). Cox regression analysis did not reveal contributing variables to LT-PFS, PFS, or OS. Conclusion:Microwave ablation appears safe, effective, and non-inferior when compared to resection and SBRT as a primary therapy for early NSCLC. Further study is warranted in comparative prospective trials.
