N2  - Introduction: Alectinib is a preferred first-line treatment option for patients with advanced ALK+ NSCLC; however, the role of alectinib and other targeted therapies in early-stage NSCLC, particularly in the neoadjuvant setting, remains unclear. NAUTIKA1 (NCT04302025) is an ongoing, phase II, umbrella trial, evaluating the efficacy and safety of multiple therapies as neo(adjuvant) treatments in patients with resectable NSCLC that meet protocol-specified biomarker criteria. Tumor markers being investigated include ALK, ROS1, NTRK, BRAF V600, RET, KRAS G12C and PD-L1. We present data from the preliminary analysis of the ALK+ cohort (neoadjuvant phase). Methods: Eligible patients were ≥18 years old, had resectable stage IB, II, IIIA or select IIIB (T3N2 only; AJCC 8th edition) ALK+ NSCLC (by immunohistochemistry, fluorescence in situ hybridization, or tissue-/blood-based next-generation sequencing), and ECOG PS 0/1. Patients were treated with neoadjuvant alectinib 600mg twice daily (BID) for eight weeks, followed by surgery; they then received up to four cycles of platinum-based chemotherapy (unless chemotherapy was refused or contraindicated), followed by alectinib 600mg BID for two years. Primary endpoint: major pathological response (MPR; local assessment). Secondary endpoints included: pathological complete response (pCR); investigator-assessed objective response rate; downstaging. Updated surgical outcomes and safety during the neoadjuvant phase are also reported. Results: Eight patients with ALK+ NSCLC enrolled by July 28, 2022 were included in this analysis. Patient demographics are listed below (Table). Median treatment duration with neoadjuvant alectinib was 8 weeks (range: 6.7-8.7). Seven eligible patients completed neoadjuvant alectinib treatment and were assessed for pathological response (Table). Five patients achieved MPR, of whom two achieved pCR. There were no delays to surgery due to adverse events (AEs) and no major intraoperative complications. Six patients had R0 resections and one patient had stable disease throughout the neoadjuvant phase, but surgeon decided not to proceed with resection due to the need for pneumonectomy. Surgical outcomes are presented in the Table. All patients reported ≥1 treatment-related AE (TRAE); most TRAEs were grade 1/2. One grade 3 TRAE (increased blood creatinine phosphokinase) was resolved by dose reduction. No patients discontinued treatment due to AEs. One patient was downstaged after neoadjuvant treatment (stage IIIB to IIIA). After surgery, three patients received adjuvant chemotherapy followed by alectinib and three received only alectinib. Conclusions: In this preliminary analysis from NAUTIKA1, neoadjuvant alectinib demonstrated major pathological response and was well tolerated in patients with ALK+ NSCLC. Further analyses with 12 patients will be available at the conference presentation. [Formula presented] Keywords: alectinib, neoadjuvant, local-regional NSCLC
