N2  - Introduction: Approximately 30% of NSCLC patients are diagnosed at early stages. Prior work has shown that patients with early-stage EGFR mutated NSCLC have an approximately 20% recurrence rate at 2 years follow up (Saw S et al, JAMA Netw Open 2021). EGFR mutations are comparatively common in never-smokers, who are not included in current CT screening guidelines. Prior work from our group highlighted the importance of reflex molecular testing in advanced disease compared with send-out testing (WCLC 2022 EP04.01-007). As part of our analysis of healthcare delivery at Northwestern Medicine in Chicago, we investigated outcomes in early-stage EGFR mutated NSCLC to explore the incidence of early-stage disease and evaluate recurrence rates. Methods: We queried the Northwestern Medicine OncoSET Molecular Database for patients with a new diagnosis of EGFR-mutant lung cancer between January 2018 and December 2022 at Northwestern Memorial Hospital. Inclusion criteria for final analysis included patients with new diagnosis of EGFR mutated NSCLC with a mutation sensitive to osimertinib who had undergone resection. Information regarding stage, date and nature of resection, mutations, receipt of chemotherapy or targeted therapy, and imaging findings prior to surgery were obtained from the medical record. Results: 58 patients diagnosed between September 2020 and December 2022 met inclusion criteria; 42 female / 16 male, 40 White / 5 Black or African American / 4 Asian / 9 other. 33 were never smokers, 25 former smokers. 11/58 patients (19%) met USPSTF screening criteria (20+ pack years and age > 50). 22 underwent lobectomy and 36 had sublobar resections. Of the 36 patients with sublobar resections, 26 had solitary nodules < 2.0 cm without visceral pleural invasion. Mutations seen were EGFR exon 21 (n=31), exon 19 deletions (n=26), and G719C + S768I (n=1). 45 had pathologic stage I, 7 stage II, and 6 stage IIIA disease. 57 patients had adenocarcinoma and 1 adenosquamous. All had negative margins at resection, and 7 had lymphovascular invasion. 9 patients received platinum doublet chemotherapy - 8 adjuvant and 1 neoadjuvant. All had stage II or III disease. 6 received osimertinib after chemotherapy. 23 patients have received adjuvant osimertinib - 2 with pathologic stage IA, 11 stage IB, 5 stage II, and 5 stage III. 11 patients had imaging findings prior to surgery described as groundglass or predominantly groundglass; 7 underwent sublobar resection and 4 lobectomy. None received chemotherapy neoadjuvant or adjuvant; 3 had adjuvant osimertinib. At a median follow up of 13.1 months as of April 13, 2023, none have experienced disease recurrence. Conclusions: 58 patients with early-stage EGFR mutated NSCLC were diagnosed and underwent resection in a 27 month span at Northwestern Medicine. None have experienced recurrence, possibly due to short duration (median 13.1 months) of follow up. Further follow up will reveal the impact of adjuvant osimertinib on outcomes. All mutations were identified on in-house reflexive NGS, indicating the utility of reflex molecular testing even in select early-stage patients. 81% of patients were diagnosed despite not being candidates for screening, highlighting an unmet need in lung cancer screening guidelines. Keywords: EGFR, early-stage, screening
