N2  - Introduction: Video-assisted thoracoscopy (VATS) has rapidly become the preferred standard surgical technique for resectable early-stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) due to faster recovery, lower pain, and aesthetically pleasing look. In patients who have traditionally received neoadjuvant therapy(NT), the approach with VATS is considered to be a relative contraindication. However, neoadjuvant therapy may increase the frequency of VATS application if it causes a reduction in tumor stage and diameter. The aim of this study was to evaluate the feasibility, safety and results of videothoracoscopic surgery in patients with NSCLC who received neoadjuvant therapy. Methods: The data of the patients who underwent videothoracoscopic anatomical lung resection(segmentectomy and greater resections) and lymph node dissection in our clinic with the diagnosis of NSCLC between January 2011 and December 2021 were prospectively recorded and analyzed retrospectively. The patients were divided into 2 groups. Group 1: included patients who did not receive oncological treatment before surgery (G1), Group 2: patients who received neoadjuvant/induction treatment before surgery (G2). Surgery was performed at least 3-6 weeks later in patients receiving neoadjuvant therapy. Prolonged air leaks lasting longer than 5 days postoperatively were considered as deaths with surgical mortality within 90 days. In the preoperative clinical council evaluation in patients who were recommended surgical treatment, a tumor greater than 5 cm in diameter was accepted as an indication for thoracotomy. Demographic characteristics, clinical stages, treatment regimens, surgical details, postoperative complications and pathological stages of the groups were compared. Results: The study included 306 patients (G1 n= 236, G2 n= 70) 241 males (78.8%), 65 females (21.2%) and a mean age of 62.6 ± 9.76 (min 26-max 81) were included. In group 2, 56 patients received chemotherapy, 7 received chemoradiotherapy, 1 received chemoradiotherapy and immunotherapy, 6 received chemotherapy and immunotherapy. Complications were detected in 101 (33%) patients after surgery (G1 n= 75, 31.8%, G2=26, 37.1%, p=0.1). Mortality was detected in five patients (1.6%) (G1 n= 3 (1.3%), G2 n=2, (2.8%), p=0.3). While the mean operation time was 162±42 minutes in Group 1, the mean operation time was 187±47 minutes in Group 2. Conversion to thoracotomy in Group 1 n=40 (%17) and n= 18 in Group 2(%25). Distant metastasis/local recurrence was seen in 24 patients in Group 1(%13), and in 14 patients in Group 2(%19). Tumor diameter was found to be greater than 5 cm before neoadjuvant therapy in 31.4% (22/70) of patients who underwent VATS lung resection after neoadjuvant therapy Conclusions: Our study has shown that lung resection can be safely performed videothoracoscopically in patients who have received neoadjuvant therapy with similar oncological results. And the study shows that neoadjuvant therapy can reduce the tumor size and increase the frequency of VATS. This can be described as a new benefit of neoadjuvant therapy. Keywords: VATS, NSCLC, Thoracic Oncology
