N2  - Introduction: Surgical resection is the gold standard for early-stage non-small cell lung cancer. In tumors ≤ 2 cm, sublobar resection may be considered in selected patients. The prognostic factors in patients with node-negative lung cancer with small size have not been well investigated. This study aims to demonstrate the prognostic factors in patients with node-negative lung adenocarcinoma ≤ 2 cm. Methods: A total of 599 patients with completely resected node-negative lung adenocarcinoma ≤ 2 cm in Taipei Veterans General Hospital from Jan 2006 to Dec 2018 were included in the study. Adenocarcinoma in situ and minimally invasive adenocarcinoma were excluded. Prognostic factors for probability of freedom from recurrence (FFR) were investigated. Results: Recurrence developed in 38 patients (6.3%) during follow-up. Univariate analysis showed that age (p = 0.005), tumor size (p < 0.001), visceral pleural invasion (p = 0.006), and the micropapillary/solid predominant pattern (p = 0.001) were significant prognostic factors for probability of FFR in node-negative lung adenocarcinoma ≤ 2 cm. Extent of pulmonary resection (wedge resection vs. anatomical resection) was not a prognostic factor for probability of FFR (p = 0.432). Those with older age (p = 0.024), greater tumor size (p = 0.002) and the micropapillary/solid predominant pattern (p = 0.005) had a significantly lower probability of FFR in multivariate analysis. The 5-year probability of FFR in patients with lepidic-predominant adenocarcinoma (n=179) was 97.5%. For patients with micropapillary/solid predominant pattern (n=75), extent of pulmonary resection (wedge resection vs. anatomical resection) was not a prognostic factor for probability of FFR (p = 0.296). Conclusions: Although patients with node-negative lung adenocarcinoma ≤ 2 cm have good prognosis after surgical resection, those with older age, larger tumor size, and histological micropapillary/solid predominant pattern have significantly higher risk for recurrence. This information is useful for identifying potential patients for adjuvant therapy. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Keywords: adenocarcinoma, prognosis, subtype
