N2  - Introduction: Salvage surgery is indicated for locoregionally recurrence or residual tumor after definitive radiotherapy and/or systemic therapy for unresectable lung cancer. This study aimed to evaluate the prognosis of patients who underwent salvage lung resection and to identify clinicopathological predictors of postoperative survival. Methods: We retrospectively reviewed clinicopathological data of 25 consecutive patients who underwent salvage lung resection from 2010 to 2022. Postoperative survival was analyzed according to the clinicopathological features, and prognostic factors were identified by using a univariate analysis. Results: The median follow-up time was 41 months (range, 2-151 months). The median age at surgery was 61 (range, 32-80), and 13 (52%) were men. The most common histologic type was adenocarcinoma, accounting for 21 patients (84%). Clinical stage IV was the most frequent initial stage and was found in 16 patients (64%), followed by stage IIIB in 4 (16%). Six (24%) received chemoradiotherapy and 19 (76%) received systemic therapy alone. Salvage lung resection was performed for residual primary tumor in 14 patients (56%) and regrowing primary tumor in 11 patients (44%). Lobectomy was the most common procedure, which was performed in 21 patients (84%), and complete R0 resection was achieved in 22 patients (88%). Grade 3 or higher postoperative complication occurred in 6 patients (24%), but there was no 30-day postoperative mortality. A major pathologic response (MPR, ≤10% of viable tumor) was observed in 6 patients (24%), and 4 patients (16%) achieved a pathological complete response. The 3-year overall and recurrence-free survival (OS and RFS) were 81.7% and 44.1%, respectively. There was no significant difference in OS between patients with and without MPR (3-year OS: 100% vs. 75.3%, p = 0.212) and residual and regrowing lesions (3-year OS: 91.7% vs. 69.3%, p = 0.181). Meanwhile, patients with high (>27.5%) stroma showed significantly better OS than those with low (≤27.5%) stroma (3-year OS: 100% vs. 44.4%, p = 0.001). Conclusions: Salvage surgery in our cohort was performed safely without postoperative mortality and showed a relatively favorable outcome. The proportion of stroma in the resected specimen may serve as a useful prognostic indicator after salvage surgery. Keywords: Non-small cell lung cancer, salvage surgery, stroma
