N2  - Introduction: Patient-reported outcomes are critical for obtaining valuable patient insight into different surgical treatment options with comparable clinical outcomes.This study aimed to compare early postoperative patient-reported symptoms and functioning between uniportal thoracoscopic lobectomy (U-VATS) and Da Vinci robotic-assisted thoracoscopic lobectomy (RATS). Methods: This study included 226 patients who underwent uniportal thoracoscopic lobectomy or Da Vinci robotic-assisted thoracoscopic lobectomy for peripheral NSCLC ≤ 2 cm in a single center prospective longitudinal study (CN-PRO-Lung 3). Symptom severity, functional status, and short-term clinical outcomes were compared between the groups. Symptom severity and functional status were measured using the Perioperative Symptom Assessment for Lung Surgery—a validated lung cancer surgery-specific scale at 1,2,3 days after surgery. Both the proportion of moderate-to-severe scores and median (range) scores on a 0-10 scale were compared between the groups. Results: Overall, During the first day of hospitalization after surgery, the degree of cough was statistically significant (P=0.009) among the 8 symptoms (pain, cough, shortness of breath, disturbed sleep, fatigue, drowsiness, distress, and difficulty walking) between U-VATS and RATS patients. However, as the hospitalization time progressed, there was no statistically significant difference in the symptoms of pain, cough, shortness of breath, disturbed sleep, fatigue, drowsiness, distress, and difficulty walking on the second and third days after surgery. In addition, there were statistical differences in terms of operative time (p=0.001), total cost of hospitalization (p=0.001), postoperative compilations (p=0.004), and number of chest tubes (p=0.001). Conclusions: For patients with early-stage peripheral non-small cell lung cancer, U-VATS and RATS lobectomy may produce similar symptom burden and functional impairment during hospitalization. In terms of hospitalization costs, operative time, and postoperative complications, U-VATS may be superior to RATS. [Formula presented] Keywords: Patient-reported outcomes, Da Vinci robotic-video-assisted thoracoscopic Lobe, Early Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer
