N2  - Introduction: In recent large-scale RCTs, tumor size and consolidation to tumor (C/T) ratio on computed tomography (CT) were considered significantly in regards to determining surgical strategy for early stage NSCLC patients. However, visceral pleural invasion (VPI) is also an important factor related to the patient's T stage, yet difficult to predict properly before surgery and discussion has been rare in previous studies. In this study, we analyzed whether VPI can be predicted through pleural CEA (pCEA), which can be quickly attained and checked intraoperatively in early stage lung adenocarcinoma patients. Methods: We retrospectively analyzed 613 patients, whom pCEA has been attained during lung resection surgery from 2017 to 2022 in a single institution. Among them, 432 clinical stage I patients diagnosed with adenocarcinoma were analyzed. The cut off value of the plural CEA suggesting VPI was confirmed through the receiver operative characteristic(ROC) curve. In addition, the analysis of preoperative and intraoperative factors that predict VPI was performed through logistic regression. Results: Of the 432 patients, 65 patients were confirmed with positive VPI (15.0%). From the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve analysis, we assumed the normal upper limit for pCEA to be 1.86 ng/mL (AUC 0.764; Sensitivity=69.2%, Specificity=74.7%, PPV=6.8%, NPV=67.4%) (Figure 1). Based on this value, the number of patients with pCEA above upper limit was 104 (33.3%). Also, according to logistic regression analysis of multi-factors, pCEA and SUVmax were significant predictors for VPI. (pCEA, OR 3.95 [2.01-7.77] P value <0.001; SUVmax, OR 4.95 [2.35-10.46] P value<0.001) (Table 1). Conclusions: Our study suggests that pCEA can be a useful factor in predicting visceral pleural invasion in early stage lung adenocarcinoma patients. According to our study, if significant positive pCEA is detected during surgery, this will imply visceral pleural invasion, and help decide which surgical plan would be beneficial. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Keywords: Pleural CEA, Visceral pleural invasion, Adenocarcinoma
