N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Cancer Case Report Posters 9 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Lung nodules are a common incidental finding on imaging, with majority being benign. However, some nodules may be malignant and require further evaluation and treatment. Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the lung (LCNEC) is a rare and highly aggressive lung cancer that has features of both small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) and non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). We present a 61-year-old female with an incidental left upper lobe pulmonary nodule s/p resection with course complicated by left lung atelectasis and bronchial stenosis requiring additional interventions. CASE PRESENTATION: A 61-year-old female with history of COPD, nicotine dependence, and rheumatoid arthritis presented with rib fracture post-mechanical fall. Incidental left upper lobe pulmonary nodule was also seen on Chest X-ray. CT Chest revealed a minimally displaced rib fracture and a 1.4 cm spiculated left upper lobe nodule corresponding to SUV 1.9 on PET-CT scan. Bronchoscopy prior to surgery revealed, a necrotic nodule with friable lymph nodes, and the left upper lobe to be severely emphysematous due to which robotic-assisted left upper lobe lobectomy with lymph node dissection was performed. Post-operative chest x-ray revealed good air expansion with the chest tube in appropriate position. Sequential Chest x-ray revealed total left lung opacification, & mediastinal shift to the left. Repeat bronchoscopy showed mucus plugging, presence of left lung atelectasis, elevation of left hemidiaphragm, and left mediastinal shift that led to the suspicion of left bronchus angulation. Patient was shifted to ICU and steroids were administered to reduce inflammation. Overnight Chest x-ray revealed left lung volume loss. Urgent left video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery revealed kinking of the left lower lobe bronchus. The lung was repositioned and a pneumoplexy with alloderm placement was performed for apical pleural tenting. A repeat bronchoscopy revealed narrowed left bronchus and a left inferior bronchus stent was inserted. The patient was successfully extubated post-procedure. The pathology report confirmed limited but high-grade large cell neuroendocrine neoplasm with extensive necrosis, aggressive with a K1-67 proliferation index of >80% and mitotic rate of >20/mm2. A plan for outpatient adjuvant chemotherapy with etoposide and cisplatin was instated for her limited but aggressive neuroendocrine tumor (T1BN0, stage 1) by Heme-oncology. The patient was successfully weaned off of oxygen support and discharged home with close pulmonology follow up. Bronchial stent was removed a week later. The patient has remained asymptomatic since then. DISCUSSION: This case highlights the importance of careful evaluation and management of lung nodules, particularly when they are found incidentally in patients with a history of smoking or other risk factors for lung cancer as early resection can improve outcomes. Additionally, the case demonstrates the potential for complications, as in our case kinking of left lower lobe bronchus which is not a common postoperative complication after right upper lobe resection requiring prompt and appropriate interventions in the postoperative period. CONCLUSIONS: In conclusion, this case report highlights the challenges and interventions required in the management of a high-grade large-cell neuroendocrine neoplasm. Early recognition and management of complications are crucial in ensuring optimal outcomes for patients undergoing lung nodule resection. REFERENCE #1: Andrini E, Marchese PV, De Biase D, Mosconi C, Siepe G, Panzuto F, Ardizzoni A, Campana D, Lamberti G. Large Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Lung: Current Understanding and Challenges. J Clin Med. 2022 Mar 7;11(5):1461. doi: 10.3390/jcm11051461. PMID: 35268551; PMCID: PMC8911276. REFERENCE #2: 1.Arai H, Tajiri M, Masuda H, et al. Intermediate bronchial kinking after right upper lobectomy for lung cancer. Asian Cardiovascular and Thoracic Annals. 2021;29(1):19-25. doi:10.1177/0218492320960325 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Farah Heis No relevant relationships by Anosh Khan No relevant relationships by Mahrukh Khan No relevant relationships by Vishakha Sirpal No relevant relationships by Sharon Weiner
