N2  - Low-grade fetal lung adenocarcinoma (L-FLAC) is a rare tumour most often affecting young females in their third to fourth decade. The histology resembles fetal lung tissue in the pseudoglandular stage of development.1 It is clinicopathologically distinct from its high-grade counterpart (H-FLAC), with differing morphology, immunohistochemical profile, molecular features and prognosis.2 L-FLAC accounts for 0.3% of all pulmonary adenocarcinomas, with H-FLAC having an incidence between 0.5–1.4%.1 We present a case of a 30-year-old female with a history of left upper lobe lung adenocarcinoma diagnosed on biopsy and treated with endobronchial debulking and chemoradiation therapy. The tumour was unsuitable for complete resection and she was followed up with regular reviews and imaging over the next 3 years. The patient became pregnant and was unable to undergo further imaging during this time, however her post-partum scan showed possible recurrence at the primary site, with subsequent fine needle aspiration confirming non-small cell lung cancer. A pneumonectomy was performed, and the 31 mm tumour showed glandular cells arranged in tubules, trabeculae and papillae with solid and fused areas. The nuclei exhibited mild to moderate atypia and mitotic figures were readily identified. Scattered squamoid morules were noted. The morphology combined with the immunohistochemical profile confirmed the diagnosis of low-grade fetal lung adenocarcinoma. Further ancillary testing showed aberrant beta-catenin expression, no overexpression of p53 and the detection of a CTNNB1 variant in the beta-catenin gene, known to be associated with low-grade FLAC. Surgery is the treatment of choice for fetal lung adenocarcinoma, with limited data available on the role of chemotherapy or radiotherapy in the neoadjuvant or adjuvant setting.3 Although low-grade FLAC generally has a good prognosis (usually due to early stage presentation)1 our case shows recurrence of this uncommon entity following multiple years of stable disease despite the encouraging initial response to chemoradiation therapy. References 1. World Health Organisation. WHO Classification of Tumours: Thoracic Tumours. 5th ed. Geneva: WHO, 2021. 2. Zhang J, Sun J, Liang X, et al. Differences between low and high grade fetal adenocarcinoma of the lung: a clinicopathological and molecular study. J Thorac Dis 2017; 9: 2071–8. 3. Lkhoyaali S, Boutayeb S, Aitelhaj M, et al. Neoadjuvant chemotherapy in well-differentiated fetal adenocarcinoma: a case report. BMC Res Notes 2014; 7: 283.
