N2  - Background: For fit patients with resectable NSCLC, surgery +/- adjuvant chemotherapy is standard of care. Despite this curative intent approach, rates of distant metastases are still substantial. To improve disease control, neoadjuvant administration of immune checkpoint inhibitors appears promising. Combining immune checkpoint inhibitors (anti PD-L1 and anti-CTLA-4) with chemotherapy and/or radiotherapy (RT) appears to further improve clinical efficacy, without unacceptable excess in toxicity. We hypothesize that neoadjuvant short course dual immunotherapy and medium dose RT improves both local and systemic disease control in resectable NSCLC with acceptable toxicity. Trial Design: DIRECT is a single center, single arm phase II study including patients with pathology proven resectable cT1c-3N0-1M0 NSCLC. Patients presenting with separate nodules in the same lobe as the primary tumor, and tumors invading surrounding structures such as thoracic wall, are excluded. Patients are treated with concurrent dual immunotherapy (durvalumab 1200mg IV on days 1 and 22, and tremelimumab 300mg IV once on day 1) and radiotherapy (3x8Gy delivered on consecutive days, first fraction before start of immunotherapy on day 1). After excluding progressive disease (RECIST), anatomical pulmonary resection is planned 4-6 weeks after the start of the neoadjuvant therapy. Study patients will be followed-up for 2 years post treatment. Primary objective is to assess safety of the neoadjuvant combination of durvalumab/tremelimumab and medium-dose RT (durva/treme/mRT) and its impact on pathological response, i.e. major pathological response percentage. Secondary objective is to assess the effect of this neoadjuvant regimen on tumor inflammation, radiological response (RECIST v1.1), metabolic response (FDG-PET), and survival outcomes (using disease free and overall survival). Exploratory objectives include the assessment of the effect of durva/treme/mRT on the immunological response of the primary tumor and the tumor-draining lymph nodes (TDLN), and to explore the effects of durva/treme/mRT and surgery on peripheral blood immune subsets distribution and phenotype. Clinical trial identification: CCMO Netherlands: NL75281.029.20. Legal entity responsible for the study: The authors. Funding: AstraZeneca. Disclosure: S. Senan: Non-Financial Interests, Personal and Institutional, Advisory Role: AstraZeneca; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: Merck; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: Cellgene; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: Ely Lilly; Other, Institutional, Funding: View Ray inc.; Other, Institutional, Funding: Varian. T. Radonic: Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: Roche; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: AbbVie; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: MSD; Other, Institutional, Funding: AbbVie. T.D. de Gruijl: Other, Personal, Advisory Board: TILT biotherapeutics; Other, Personal, Advisory Board: DC Prima; Financial Interests, Personal, Funding: Macrophage Farma; Financial Interests, Personal, Funding: Idera Pharmaceutical. I. Bahce: Non-Financial Interests, Personal and Institutional, Advisory Role: AstraZeneca; Non-Financial Interests, Personal and Institutional, Advisory Role: Boehringer; Non-Financial Interests, Personal and Institutional, Advisory Role: MSD; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: Pfizer; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: Takeda; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: Cellgene; Non-Financial Interests, Personal and Institutional, Advisory Role: Roche; Non-Financial Interests, Personal and Institutional, Advisory Role: Bristol Myers Squibb; Non-Financial Interests, Personal, Advisory Role: Eli Lilly. All other authors have declared no conflicts of interest.
