N2  - Background: NCCN guidelines for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) establish an evidence-based foundation for management of patients with NSCLC. We seek to evaluate the extent of surgery for patients undergoing resection of the lung for NSCLC at a community oncology cancer center. Methods: Retrospective review of the patients undergoing surgery for a diagnosis of NSCLC between 01/01/2015 through 12/31/2016 that included any type of resection that was done. Data was analyzed by IBM SPSS v.20. Results: Total number of patients undergoing thoracic surgery for a diagnosis of NSCLC between 01/01/2015 through 12/31/2016 was 50. Out of the total of 50 patients, 11 (22%) underwent wedge resection (WDG), 37 (74%) underwent lobectomy (LOB) and 2 (4%) underwent pneumonectomy. The median age of the patient cohort was 68.5 years. Median age of patients undergoing LOB and WDG was 71 years and 60 years respectively. 14 patients underwent surgery for left-sided and 36 for right-sided tumors. 30 lobectomies were done on the right compared to only 7 on the left. Histology was adenocarcinoma in 31 patients (61%), squamous cell carcinoma in 15 patients (30%), adeno-squamous carcinoma in 2 patients, large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma in 1 patient and poorly differentiated carcinoma in 1 patient. This distribution was similar across patients undergoing LOB & WDG. Median size of the primary tumor was 2.55 cm. Patients undergoing WDG (1.8 cm) had smaller tumors compared to LOB (2.6 cm). Majority (>90%) of the patients undergoing surgery were either stage I or stage II with more early stage (stage I) tumors in WDG cohort (91%). Median number of lymph nodes (LN) sampled was 8. More lymph nodes were sampled during LOB. Median of 3 N2 nodal stations were sampled during LOB. Most common N2 stations sampled was subcarinal (station 7; 37 patients) followed by pulmonary ligament (station 2R; 28 patients) and right lower paratracheal (station 4R; 20 patients). Only 21 patients (57%) undergoing LOB had 3 or more N2 nodal stations sampled. Subcarinal (station 7) was the most common in right-sided and left-sided tumors in 90% and 56% of cases respectively. No LN were sampled from left lower paratracheal (4L) station in left-lung lobectomies. Conclusions: WDG tends to downstage the cancer due to inadequate sampling of the N2 station LN. Higher proportion of patients underwent LOB for right sided tumors compared to left. Sampling of N2 nodal stations may be suboptimal in a community setting.
