N2  - Introduction: The impact of left digit bias is well described in behavioral economics (e.g. price evaluations of potato chips at $2.99 instead of $3.00). The impact of left digit bias in cancer treatment has not been previously characterized. The objective of this study is to evaluate whether left digit bias affects surgical decision-making for patients with early-stage non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC) who are 79 versus 80 years old. Methods: Practice patterns and outcomes for patients 79, 80 and 81 years with T1-T2N0M0 (NSCLC) in the National Cancer Data Base (2004-2015) were analyzed using multivariable logistic regression, Cox proportional hazards modeling and propensity score-matched analysis (matched on 14 common prognostic covariates). Results: Patients 80 years old were less likely to receive an anatomic lung resection when compared to patients 79 years old (multivariable adjusted OR: 0.82 [95% CI: 0.72-0.94], p=0.003). There was no significant difference in receiving anatomic lung resection between patients 80 and 81 years old (multivariable adjusted OR: 0.94 [95% CI: 0.81-1.08], p=0.35). In multivariable Cox proportional hazards modeling, there was no difference in overall survival between patients 79 and 80 years (HR: 1.08 [95% CI: 0.98-1.19], p=0.10) and between patients 80 and 81 years old (HR: 0.95 [95% CI: 0.84-1.08], p=0.43). In propensity score-matched analysis of 79- and 80-year-old patients and of 80- and 81-year-old patients undergoing anatomic lung resection, there were no differences between the groups with regard to 30-day readmission, 30-day mortality, 90-day mortality, median and 5-year survival (Table, Figure). [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Conclusion: Patients 80 years old with early-stage NSCLC are less likely to receive anatomic lung resection when compared to patients 79 years old, despite there being no significant difference in short- and long-term outcomes between these two age groups. This apparent left digit bias needs to be recognized to improve clinical decision-making for patients with lung cancer. Keywords: lung cancer, bias, Surgery
