N2  - Introduction: Surgery remains a cornerstone of cure for early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). The value of systemic therapy for node negative early stage lung cancer is unclear, yet local and distant recurrence rates remain high after surgery. The advent of immunotherapy for advanced NSCLC has created new opportunities for patients. With better side effect profiles, improved outcomes and prolonged survival, immune checkpoint inhibition (ICI) as a single agent or in combination has become a first-line therapy in advanced NSCLC. Emerging data indicate that these advantages may be translated to early stage NSCLC. Previous studies have demonstrated safety and tolerability of neoadjuvant ICI for patients with operable NSCLC, as well as unprecedented major pathological response (MPR) rates, ranging from 20 to 75%. However, the ideal timing and regimen remain unknown. It is also unknown how the degree of pathological response should impact the extent of surgical resection. Determining the likelihood of complete pathological response (pCR) prior to surgery through clinically available biomarkers is therefore key. Radiomic and ctDNA technologies are attractive for this purpose. This phase II study aims to evaluate the use of clinically available biomarkers during neoadjuvant therapy with pembrolizumab alone or in combination with chemotherapy for stage IA3-IIA operable NSCLC to predict the likelihood of pCR. Furthermore, we aim to derive predictive data from single-cell RNA sequencing transcriptomic signatures and imaging mass cytometry tumor immune microenvironment signatures, and correlate these to ctDNA resolution and pCR. Together, these strategies may allow for more limited planned surgical resection strategies and greatly inform the care of patients with advanced NSCLC. Methods: This is a single-site prospective randomized open-label phase II trial enrolling patients aged ≥18 years with previously untreated, operable, histologically confirmed stages IA3-IIA NSCLC (AJCC 8th ed), ECOG 0-1, no immunodeficiency, no active pregnancy and available tumor biopsy samples for PD-L1 testing. 44 participants will be randomly assigned in a 1:1 ratio between 3 cycles of neoadjuvant pembrolizumab 200 mg IV Q3W (Arm A) vs. neoadjuvant pembrolizumab 200 mg IV Q3W with platinum doublet chemotherapy (carboplatin AUC 6 900 mg with paclitaxel 200 mg/m2 [squamous and mixed histology] vs. carboplatin AUC 5 750 mg with pemetrexed 500 mg/m2 [non-squamous histology]) (Arm B). All patients will be offered adjuvant pembrolizumab 400 mg Q6W for 6 cycles. Based on tumor board assessment, standard of care adjuvant chemotherapy will be offered only if indicated. Randomization will be stratified by PD-L1 tumor proportion score (<50%, ≥50%). An anatomical lung resection (pneumonectomy, lobectomy or segmentectomy) is required at surgery with complete mediastinal lymph node dissection. All participants will undergo longitudinal ctDNA measurement. Response will also be assessed with imaging (diffusion-weighted MRI, CT and PET). Primary endpoint is ctDNA resolution during neoadjuvant therapy. Secondary endpoints are MPR (≤10% viable tumor cells in resected primary tumor/lymph nodes), pCR (no residual invasive cancer in resected lung specimen/lymph nodes), safety (CTCAE v5.0 or later) and imaging scores. Exploratory endpoints include overall survival (randomization to all-cause death) and disease-free survival (randomization to first of disease progression, unresectable tumor, recurrence or death). Keywords: Immune Checkpoint Inhibition, non-small cell lung cancer, neoadjuvant therapy
