N2  - Purpose/Objective(s): Development of another lung primary can be common following the successful treatment of lung cancer. It is unclear how the outcomes of these patients compare to those without a history of lung cancer after repeat definitive therapy. We evaluated the survival of patients with recurrent/de novo early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) who underwent definitive stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) and had a history of previously treated NSCLC. Materials/Methods: We evaluated the charts of 563 patients who underwent thoracic SBRT from February 2007 to April 2019, identifying 84 patients who received definitive SBRT for early stage NSCLC and had history of previously treated NSCLC. Pertinent clinicopathologic data were recorded for the treatment of both malignancies. Survival endpoints were calculated via univariate and multivariate analysis as well Kaplan-Meier log-rank testing performed in R Results: Within this cohort, the median follow-up was 31.4 months; median interval from prior lung cancer treatment to SBRT was 36.8 months; most common initial treatment was lobectomy (70.2%) followed by wedge resection (16.7%); 78.6% initially had T1-2N0 disease whereas 97.6% who underwent definitive SBRT were T1-2N0. Compared to their initial malignancies, 56.0% developed tumors on the ipsilateral side and 76.2% had a similar histology to their initial tumor. On definitive SBRT, most commonly patients underwent a single fraction treatment of 27-34 Gray (Gy) (36.9%) or 54-60Gy in 3 fractions (33.3%). Within the follow up period, there were 34 (40.4%) relapses and 44 (52.4%) deaths Kaplan-Meier estimated median relapse-free survival (RFS) and overall survival (OS) was 61.1 months and 52.3 months respectively. Furthermore, within a dataset of 440 patients who underwent SBRT for early stage NSCLC, history of prior lung cancer was associated with a favorable overall survival on univariate and multivariate (HR 0.70, 95% CI 0.50-0.96, p = 0.028) analysis. Conclusion: Despite a history of previous definitive therapy for a lung malignancy, those with another lung primary treated with SBRT have a favorable outcome.
