N2  - Introduction/Aim. Rehabilitation is not currently part of standard care for individuals with lung cancer, either pre or postoperatively. The current study aimed to determine if a prehabilitation program for individuals undergoing lung cancer surgery was safe and feasible and gain further insight into the lung cancer patients experience with prehabilitation. Method. Mixed method feasibility study consisting of a pilot randomized feasibility trial and qualitative interviews. Eligible participants included adults with suspected Stage I-IIIA non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) with operable disease. The intervention consisted of education and a preoperative centre or home-based program of aerobic and resistance exercise performed for 4-8 weeks prior to lung resection surgery: Initially delivered face-to-face then delivered either as supervised sessions or at home with weekly telephone calls. The primary endpoint was feasibility, including rates of consent, dropout, adverse events and safety. Secondary outcomes included number of post-operative pulmonary complications, hospital length of stay and patient reported outcomes. Outcomes were measured at baseline, prior to surgery, on hospital discharge and at 30-days post-surgery. Qualitative interviews were conducted at the conclusion of the program. Results. Twenty-four patients were included:11 patients were randomized to prehabilitation. The preoperative exercise intervention was safe and feasible, no adverse events were recorded. Rate of consent was 39% with a 40% rate of dropouts. The mean (standard deviation) time from lung cancer diagnosis to surgery was 58.8 (25.9)days. The mean adherence to the exercise intervention was 95.6%. Patients reported satisfaction with the prehabilitation program. Barriers to prehabilitation included; patient comorbidities, travel, symptoms and motivation. There were no other significant between group findings. Conclusion. Prehabilitation in lung cancer surgery is safe and feasible and well tolerated by patients with varying symptom burden.
