N2  - Rationale: Stereotactic body radiotherapy, (SBRT) has emerged as an alternative to a limited resection for treatment of Stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) in patients unable to undergo lobectomy. Many of SBRT-treated patients are at high risk for lobectomy due to borderline lung function as a consequence of underlying chronic obstructive lung disease (COPD). In this setting, SBRT is thought to provide treatment for early stage lung cancer while preserving lung function; however, empirical data are limited. Due to the importance on functional status and quality of life, we evaluated lung function pre vs. post SBRT in patients with stage I NSCLC. Methods: We conducted a prospective, observational cohort study of subjects with stage II NSCLC, who were not candidates for lobectomy. Participants underwent a baseline in person interview. Spirometry was obtained at baseline and 6 months post SBRT per ATS/ERS recommendations. We obtained measurements of percent predicted forced expiratory volume in 1 second (ppFEV1) and forced expiratory volume in 1 second/forced vital capacity, (FEV1/FVC) ratio. Paired T-Tests were used to compare pre vs. post SBRT ppFEV1 and FEV1/FVC ratio. Results: We included data from 146 stage I NSCLC treated with SBRT. Mean (SD) age was 74 (10) years, 50% were male, 75% white, 11% black, 2% Hispanic, and 24% from other race. Almost 60% were former smokers and 19% current smokers; mean (SD) pack years of smoking was 53 (31). Of the 109 participants with complete baseline spirometry data: mean (SD) ppFEV1 was 67.2% (28.0%), mean FEV1/FVC was 59.4% (16.4%). Almost half (47%) of participants had COPD; 29% had ppFEV1 50%. Of the 80 subjects that reached the six month follow-up time point assessment, 45 had repeat spirometry performed. Mean ppFEV1 among these subjects was 69.1% (25.7%); mean difference 7.3, p-value: 0.02. Mean FEV1/FVC ratio was 46.6% (18.8%), mean difference -0.6, p-value: 0.79. Conclusions: Among patients unable to undergo lobectomy, there were minor, non-clinically significant changes in lung function observed 6 months following SBRT for early stage NSCLC. These data suggest that SBRT can be safely delivered to patients with limited lung function.
