N2  - Introduction: As new screening guidelines for lung cancer disseminate into practice, there is likely to be substantive increase in the detection rate of early stage lung cancer. As payment models move from traditional fee for service towards payment “bundles” for care episodes, a deeper understanding of costs associated with stage I lung cancer surgery becomes increasingly relevant. Methods: We aimed to characterize hospital-level variation in Medicare expenditure after surgical resection for stage I non-small cell lung carcinoma. We performed a retrospective study using the Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results-Medicare linked database to determine costs of care in the 90 days after hospitalization for stage I lung cancer resection. Our main outcome was hospital risk standardized cost (RSC) of care. Additionally, we analyzed contributions of each component of cost on overall RSC variation. Results: There were 6,459 surgical resections at 591 hospitals. Most procedures were lobectomies (85%); half were minimally invasive. The median risk standardized cost (RSC) for a patient-centered surgical episode was $24,674. Both the index hospitalization (p < 0.001) and outpatient costs (p < 0.001) were associated with higher overall RSCs. The index admission accounted for 55% of the total costs. Of patients who received post-acute care, the largest cost contributor was a stay in long-term acute care. Conclusion: As we see an increasing number of early stage lung cancer diagnoses, it will be important to understand cost implications of treatment. Our study suggests that while other costs do play a role, high costs during the index admission lead to high cost overall.
