N2  - Purpose/Objective(s): Lung cancer is a leading cause of cancer related death. The incidence of early stage NSCLC is likely to rise in the future and because of the scarcity of resources, cost-effectiveness evaluation of therapeutic alternatives is mandatory. We previously published a cost-effectiveness analysis of SBRT and lobectomy in early stage NSCLC in a French payer perspective. In this work, we developed a graphical user interface (GUI), to enable similar analysis in other health system perspective. Materials/Methods: We designed a Markov model to simulate the clinical trajectory of a patient with medically operable stage I NSCLC. Markov simulation is a statistical method that allows the simulation of the transition of a hypothetical cohort of patients between different health states in fixed increments of time. In both SBRT and VATS lobectomy, the patient enters the model in the progression free survival (PFS) health state after receiving the treatment. After a fixed time increment, the patient could either stay in the PFS state or progress to recurrence state, which could be local (LR), regional (RR) or distant (DR), or to death. From the recurrent state, the patient could stay in the same health state or progress to death. We assumed that the initial staging of the patient, before treatment, did not find evidence of nodal disease. SBRT was delivered ambulatory in 1 to 8 fractions and the patient was exposed to a risk of grade 2 or 3 pneumonitis and to chest wall pain. VATS lobectomy was associated with a mortality risk and a risk of chemotherapy according to nodal pathologic evaluation and a risk of complications. Moreover, patients undergoing lobectomy had an immediate risk of pulmonary infection, of chest pain and of dyspnea. We derived survival probabilities and recurrence probabilities from the pooled analysis of STARS and ROSEL studies. We projected utilities and costs for SBRT and VATS lobectomy and calculated the increment cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER) over a lifetime horizon with an annual discounted rate of 4% for both utilities and costs. We assessed the influence of the assumptions made in the model in deterministic sensitivity analysis (DSA). Finally, the overall model uncertainty was tested in the probabilistic sensitivity analysis (PSA), in which we drew 100 random values from specified probability distributions for each parameter. The number of simulation could be increased on request. Results: We developed a Graphical User Interface (GUI) which allows the user to set the costs used to run the model in order to fit a specific perspective of analysis. The GUI delivers read-to-use plot, data frame and a report of the cost-effectiveness analysis. Conclusion: While building a Markov model usually requires specific skills and a significant amount of work, we provide a convenient, ready to use and customizable solution for anyone willing to conduct a medico-economic evaluation of SBRT and lobectomy in early stage NSCLC.
