N2  - RATIONALE: Minimally invasive surgery has improved the treatment of surgical patients. These surgical techniques minimize trauma and optimize patient recovery without compromising the surgical outcome. Over the last two decades, growing evidence has suggested that Videoassisted Thoracic Surgery (VATS) lobectomy is an alternative approach for patients with earlystage non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC), for its benefits in terms of decreased acute postoperative pain, reduced cytokine release, less impairment in pulmonary function, shorter chest tube duration, and consequently, shorter hospital stay. However, this technique has not yet gained wide-spread acceptance in the surgical community, mainly because of the uncertain oncologic efficacy of VATS, especially regarding its potential for recurrence. METHODS: A retrospective review was conducted of 55 consecutive patients undergoing lobectomy for lung cancer at our center, from November 2012 to October 2016. Uniportal VATS lobectomy was performed using anatomic hilar dissection and complete mediastinal lymphadenectomy, through a 3-5cm single skin incision without ribs spreading. The total follow up time was 72 months. RESULTS: 20 men (36.4%) and 35 women with a mean age of 63.3 years (31-81 years) were operated. The postoperative staging was as follows: stage I, 37 patients (67.3%); stage II, 11 patients; stage III, 4 patients; stage IV, 1 patient; SCLC (small-cell lung cancer) limited disease, 2 patients. 36.4% patients were submitted to adjuvant chemotherapy and 7.2% to neoadjuvant chemotherapy. The mean follow up time was 52.2 months. The 1, 2 and 3-year overall survival (OS) rate was 100%, 94.5% and 87.3%. The global OS, local recurrence rate and distant metastases rate were 87.3%, 14.5% and 12.7% at end of follow up (72 months). The stage I NSCLC patients had an overall survival of 100% and a recurrence free survival of 94.6% at end of follow up. CONCLUSION: Our single center series corroborates recent studies that introduced VATS lobectomy with mediastinal lymphadenectomy as an alternative procedure to open surgery with at least equal oncologic efficacy (overall survival and locoregional recurrence), beyond the already recognized benefits in terms of safety, reduced postoperative complications and shorter recovery period.
