N2  - Background: Lobectomy with lymph node dissection remains the standard of care for the treatment of stage 1, non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). Whereas wedge resection represents a viable surgical option, limited data are available to quantify optimal margin distance with regard to risk of recurrence and survival. Some studies suggest an increase in recurrence rates when comparing wedge resection to lobectomy. Clinical judgment based on comorbidities, as well as tumor location and size of lesion remain the main factors in determining the most optimal course of treatment. Our study aims to investigate the relationship between margin distance and local recurrence risk, as well as overall survival outcomes with the goal of preserving a maximal amount of lung tissue without compromising outcomes. Methods: This investigation included patients who underwent wedge resection for stage 1, NSCLC at Stony Brook University Hospital's Lung Cancer Evaluation Center from January 2001 to May 2013. The exclusion criteria included other active malignancies, lymph node or distant metastases at diagnosis, previous chemotherapy or radiotherapy. Cox Proportional Hazard Models are used to examine the relationship between margin distance and local recurrence, in addition to overall survival, while adjusting for comorbidities and pulmonary function testing, smoking history, tumor size, tumor location, distant location from pleura and location from fissure, as well as whether the lymph nodes were sampled. Results: This study will includes 225 stage 1, NSCLC patients treated by surgical wedge resection. More data to follow Conclusion: Preliminary data suggests a relationship between margin size and recurrence rates.
