N2  - Rationale: Surgical resection remains the treatment of choice for resectable lung cancer (LCa). Despite this, tumour recurrence is the most common cause of treatment failure after surgery. The renin-angiotensin system is a primary regulator of blood pressure that also plays a role in cell proliferation, immunoinflammatory response, hypoxia and angiogenesis, which are critical biological processes in lung cancer. Previous studies reported that angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors (ACEi) or antagonists of angiotensin II type 1 receptor (AATR1) may provide enhanced efficacy for LCa in combination with chemotherapeutics albeit conflicting results, namely in concern with long term ACEi intake. However, their impact on relevant clinical outcomes in LCa patients at early stages remains controversial. Therefore, we sought to analyse whether, beyond antihypertensive drugs intake, the time of exposure to these drugs is associated with prognosis of early stage LCa. Methods: A retrospective cohort study was performed on patients who had undergone lobectomy for LCa (stages IA-IIIA), in a University Hospital, from January 2008 to December 2014. The patients had the last follow-up consecutively between April 2014 and July 2018. Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS. Cumulative hazard and survival curves were plotted with Kaplan-Meier and compared using Log-rank test. Results: LCa patients (n=216, 68.1% males), with a mean age of 63.4±10.2 years, had predominantly adenocarcinoma histological subtype (67.6%), 20.2% were squamous and 12.1% other subtypes. Patients that have undergone higher therapeutic burden (lobectomy plus chemotherapy and radiotherapy), followed by those submitted to surgery plus chemotherapy, were at higher risk for disease progression, and had lower time-to-progression (Kaplan-Meier, p<0.0001) than those treated only with surgery. Furthermore, our group found that antihypertensive drugs intake had no association with progression free survival (PFS) (p=0.944) or overall survival (OS) (p=0.511). Moreover there were no differences between the group of patients under ACEi or AATR1. Noteworthy, when categorized patients on antihypertensive drugs intake according to tertiles of time of exposure, there was a significantly lower time-toprogression (Log rank test, P<0.0001) and time-to-death of lower tertile groups (Log rank test, P<0.0001), regardless of whether the patients were taking ACEi or AATR1. Conclusion: In this study, albeit antihypertensive drugs intake was not related with PFS and OS in early stage LCa patients, there was an important association between the time of exposure to ACEi and AATR1 and major clinical endpoints, in a cumulative time-dependent manner. These findings should foster research on the magnitude of antihypertensive drugs exposure in LCa. (Figure Preseted) .
