N2  - Rationale: Interstitial lung disease (ILD) is characterized by damage to the lung parenchyma due to inflammation and/or fibrosis1. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) and other diseases that affect the lung interstitium have been associated with lung carcinogenesis2. Approximately 10% of patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) will also have an ILD diagnosis3-4. Lung cancers in patients with IPF predominate in the subpleural lower lobe regions and consist of squamous cell subtypes5-6. The clinocopathological characteristics of lung cancer in non-IPF ILD and other granulomatous diffuse parenchymal lung disease are poorly understood. Methods: Patients with a biopsy-proven diagnosis of an ILD other than IPF who underwent surgical resection of a primary lung neoplasm from January 2000 to May 2018 were included in this study. Patients were classified into three groups: chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP), sarcoidosis, and non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP). Clinical and radiographic information was obtained from their medical records. Results: Twenty total cases of concomitant non-IPF ILD and malignancy were identified with the majority being in patients with confirmed HP. Adenocarcinoma (ADCA) was the most common histology (Table 1) and most were identified as early stage NSCLCs. The majority of tumors in patients with HP originated from a peripheral, subpleural area that on gross inspection correlated with areas of fibrosis on CT scans obtained prior to their malignancy (Table 2). There was no clear anatomic radiologic correlation between the tumors found in the sarcoid and in the NSIP groups. Among the 12 patients with ADCA, a KRAS mutation was observed in 3 (25%) and BRAF in 1 (8%); none were EGFR or ALK positive. Among the patients who underwent curative lung resections for early stage lung cancer, there was not a major decrement in their post-operative lung function as assessed by forced vital capacity (Table 3). Conclusions: In this cohort of patients with early stage lung malignancies and biopsy-proven, non-IPF ILD, ADCA was the most common subtype of NSCLC. Tumors in patients with HP originated in areas with corresponding subpleural fibrosis and were basilar predominant. No such trend was noted in the patients with NSIP and sarcoid. The relatively small decrement in FVC observed following resection suggests that surgery remains a viable option for patients with early stage NSCLC and non-IPF ILD. No definitive conclusion can be made about the mutation status given the small sample size.
