N2  - Introduction: Surgical complications following pulmonary resection with video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) for early stage lung cancer may present initially to the interventional pulmonologist. We present a rare post-VATS bronchial complication diagnosed with bronchoscopy. Case Description: A 65 year-old woman with a history of prior left upper lobectomy for early stage non-small cell lung cancer presented to our hospital with fever, chills and a loculated pleural effusion. One week earlier she underwent elective VATS for a highly suspicious right lower lobe lung nodule. The nodule was wedge-resected and onsite pathology was positive for adenocarcinoma, with subsequent mediastinal lymphadenectomy revealing all negative results. The thoracic surgical team then proceeded with a basilar wedge resection, reportedly sparing the right lower lobe superior segment and its vascular supply, with hopes of preserving all possible lung function given her history of prior left upper lobectomy. She now returns one week later with worsening dyspnea and cough. After careful review of her current computed tomography (CT) chest imaging, we noted consolidative parenchyma of the superior segment distal to a hyperlucency in this location. Diagnostic bronchoscopy revealed a staple line across the right lower lobe superior segment (picture 1). Our findings were discussed with the surgical team and the patient proceeded to the operating room where the right lower lobe superior segment was found to be pus-filled and necrotic. A completion right lower lobectomy with debridement and irrigation was performed and the patient did well post-operatively. Discussion: Catastrophic VATS complications have been described as those that require an additional unplanned surgical procedure. Such complications are uncommon with the literature reporting a less than 2% rate. Vascular and bronchial inadvertent ligation is extremely rare but has been reported and is usually noticed immediately. In our case, the superior segment of the right lower lobe and its vascular supply was unintentionally stapled, leading to the post-obstructive infectious presentation one week later. The diagnosis is made with a careful review of all airways of interest on imaging, as well as a thorough review of the operative report and discussion with the surgical team. This case highlights the importance of a strong working relationship between the interventional pulmonologist and thoracic surgeon that ultimately lead to a successful outcome of a potential fatal complication.
