N2  - Purpose: Lung resections via a video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) approach are becoming increasingly popular. While recent studies have suggested that VATS lobectomies are non-inferior to thoracotomy for treating early stage non-small cell lung cancer, these studies have largely been performed in specialist thoracic centres where the learning curve for VATS techniques has been surmounted. This study described the outcomes of the first 124 consecutive cases carried out by a single (predominantly cardiac) surgeon in a non-specialist regional centre. Methodology: A retrospective review of prospectively collected data was performed for all patients intended to undergo VATS for lung resections by a single surgeon between 2012–2018 (n = 124) in Geelong, Victoria. Surgeries included lobectomies, sub-lobar anatomical resections, bi-lobectomies, and pneumonectomies for all indications, including benign or malignant pathologies. Results: In-hospital and 30-day mortality was 0.8%. The median duration of procedure was 170 minutes, with a decreasing trend over the case series. Conversion to thoracotomy rate was 5.6% (n = 7). Median length of hospital stay was 5 days. Complications included sputum retention (3.2%, n = 4), prolonged air leak >7 days (9.7%, n = 12), pneumonia (3.2%, n = 4), empyema (0.8%, n = 1), re-operation (1.6% n = 2), and reintubation (2.4%, n = 3). Conclusion: Lung resections via a VATS approach can be learnt and performed with low complication and mortality rates. Outcomes are comparable to those established in the literature, with events occurring early in the learning curve.
