N2  - Objective: According to the Surveillance Epidemiology End Results database, 30% of elderly patients are offered a palliative treatment or denied surgery compared to 8%of the youngest patients. The aim of our study was to establish the safety and feasibility of robotic lobectomy and lymphadenectomy in elderly patients with non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). Methods:We retrospectively collected and analyzed data fromall elderly patients (>75 years) who underwent a robotic major lung resection for NSCLC in our institution between November 2010 and November 2017. Age, comorbidities, diagnosis, gender, type and duration of surgery, conversion rate, duration of stay, and early and late complications were reviewed retrospectively and analyzed descriptively. The Charlson comorbidity index was used to evaluate the weightiness of comorbidities. Results: Sixty-nine patients (male/female = 45/24) with a mean age of 77.3 years (range, 75-85 years) underwent robotic lobectomy for NSCLC. A Charlson comorbidity index >2 was found in 34% of patients. No postoperative deaths occurred. The overall complication rate was 23.2%, with a major complication rate of 2.9% (2/69 prolonged air leaks that required reintervention). The median length of hospitalization was 6 days. A pathological stage I tumor was found in 46 patients, II in 15, III in 7, and IV in 1. The mean and median numbers of resected lymph nodes were 14 and 13. Conclusions: In elderly patients with NSCLC, the treatment decision should never be based on the chronological age alone. Robotic surgery shows a low mortality rate, reduced surgical trauma, and a shorter recovery time. It improves the quality of life, decreases postoperative delirium, and offers an effective and safe opportunity to treat elderly patients affected by NSCLC. (Figure Presented) .
