N2  - INTRODUCTION: The use of robotic lobectomy for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) has improved beyond the initial experience. We sought to examine perioperative and long-term outcomes of robotic lobectomy compared to open- and video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) at a high volume center. METHODS: Records of patients undergoing lobectomy for NSCLC between 2012 and 2017 were reviewed. Perioperative outcomes, overall- (OS) and recurrence-free survival (RFS) were compared. Propensity score were used to adjust for selection bias in a multivariate Cox regression analysis. RESULTS: Lobectomy was performed in 527 NSCLC patients (robotic 221, open 191, VATS 113). Most patients were stage I or II (92%). Robotic lobectomy patients were older (66 vs 63 years, p=0.008) and had worse pulmonary function than VATS patients (diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide 73% vs 87%, p=0.001). Overall morbidity (30% vs 49% vs 24%, p=0.001) and length of stay (5.9 vs 8.3 vs 5.6 days, p=0.001) were similar between robotic and VATS patients and worse following open lobectomy. Unadjusted OS and RFS was similar by stage. On multivariate analysis, adjusted for propensity scores, stage, histology and margin status, there was no difference in OS or RFS between robotic and open (OS, hazard ratio [HR] 1.02 [0.61-1.70]; RFS, HR 0.85 [0.56-1.28]) or VATS lobectomy (OS, HR 1.31 [0.74-2.32]; RFS, HR 1. 07 [0.66-1.73]). CONCLUSIONS: A robotic lobectomy has been utilized for higher-risk patients, and has resulted in comparable short-term outcomes to VATS approach. Accounting for differences in patient selection, robotic lobectomy has the potential for similar survival and disease control, as compared to open or VATS approach.
