N2  - Background: Among patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), those with resected stage I disease have a favorable prognosis. There exists no consensus about the ideal duration of postoperative routine follow-up. The aim of this study was to determine a reasonable duration of aftercare in this population. Methods: The complete data of 300 consecutive patients with pathologic stage I NSCLC, resected curatively between 2005 and 2015, were evaluated retrospectively. Results: The median age was 66 years (60.1-72.2), the majority was male (n = 187; 62%). Lobectomy was performed in 284 cases (95%), adenocarcinoma was evident in 191 patients (64%). During a median follow-up of 3.2 years there were 59 recurrences (19.7%) and 38 patients died from cancer (12.7%). In competing risk analysis, the cumulative incidences of recurrence at 1, 5, and 10 years after surgery were 7% (95%CI: 4-10), 27% (20-34), and 36% (26-47). Corresponding risks of deathfrom- cancer were 2% (1-4), 16% (11-21), and 30% (19-41), and of death-from-other-causes were 3% (2-6), 12% (8-18), and 42% (24-59). Flexible parametric modeling revealed that the rates of recurrence and death-from-cancer peaked after 1 and 3 years postoperatively, but remained constantly elevated well beyond 5 years. After 5 postoperative years, the rate of death-fromother- causes exceeded the rates of recurrence and death-fromcancer. Conclusion: In patients with stage I NSCLC, rates of recurrence and death-from-cancer are persistently elevated beyond 5 years after surgery. These patients may benefit from structured long-term aftercare. The absolute risk of death-from-othercauses was similarly high as the risk-of-death-from-cancer.
