N2  - Introduction: As lung cancer screening has increased the rate of diagnosing smaller lung tumors, surgeons have re-evaluated the necessity of lobectomy as gold standard for pulmonary resection. We sought to compare wedge resection to lobectomy to identify both differences in patients in whom they are performed as well as short- and long-term outcomes. Methods: All patients age > 65 years in the Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results - Medicare (SEER-Medicare) database undergoing lobectomy or wedge resection between 2007 and 2010 for stage I non-small cell lung cancers < 2cm were evaluated. Chi square, Wilcoxon rank sum, and logistic regression were used to look for associations between technique (wedge or lobectomy) and clinical predictors. Multivariable models investigated the independent effect of technique on rates of nodal sampling/dissection, complication, blood transfusion, prolonged hospital stay, and longterm mortality. Results: There were 1,186 patients: 788 (66.4%) lobectomy and 398 (33.6%) wedge resection. Wedge resection was associated with older age (p=<0.0001), smaller tumor (p= <0.0001), upper lobe location (p=0.0274), squamous histology (p=0.011), higher comorbidity index (p=<0.0001), and video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (p<0.0001). In the adjusted model, wedge resection was associated with squamous histology (ORadj 1.51, 95% CI: 1.14-2.00), higher comorbidity index (ORadj 1.24, 95% CI: 1.11-1.38), older age (ORadj 1.05, 95% CI: 1.09-1.08). Wedge resection was less common in middle (vs. upper) lobe tumors (ORadj 0.32, 95% CI: 0.16-0.63) and for larger (11-20mm vs. < 10mm) tumors (ORadj 0.40, 95% CI: 0.28-0.57). On multivariable analysis, wedge resection was associated with lower rates pathologic node evaluation (ORadj 0.02, 95% CI: 0.01-0.04), complications (ORadj 0.53, 95% CI: 0.41-0.70), and prolonged (>7 days) length of stay (ORadj 0.42, 95% CI: 0.31-0.57). Rates of blood transfusion (ORadj.066, 95% CI: 0.40-1.09) and long-term mortality (ORadj 0.80, 95% CI: 0.59-1.08) were not significantly different. Conclusion: The decision on the type of resection (wedge vs. lobectomy) for small early stage lung cancers depends upon clinical characteristics. When adjusted for differences, wedge resection patients have better short-term outcomes and no worse long-term mortality than lobectomy, perhaps due to better tolerated and equally effective surgery. With fewer wedge resection patients undergoing nodal sampling/dissection, this cohort may have been under-staged and long-term mortality would be better if occult higher stage patients could have been excluded. These data suggest wedge resection might be a better operation for small lung cancers, but proper pathologic node staging is imperative to evaluate the long-term impact of this technique.
