N2  - Introduction: The proposals for 8th TNM staging for lung cancer is published and going to be used worldwide after January 1st, 2018. In this study we aimed to evaluate 8th TNM staging system in a retrospective cohort of patients with early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) who received adjuvant chemotherapy. Method: The study included a retrospective cohort 100 patients with early stage NSCLC. All of the patients had undergone surgery between the years 2004 and 2014 and received adjuvant cisplatin based chemotherapy after surgical resection. Median follow-up time was 44 months (range:1-144 month). T stage, N stage, N category using new descriptors (N1a, N1b, N2a, N2b), histological type, the presence of visceral pleural invasion, perineural invasion, vascular invasion, gender, age, weight loss, ECOG performance status were examined for prognostic significance using Cox's regression analysis. Results: There were 86 male, 14 female patients with a mean age of 55.4±8.9 (range: 30-76) years. Surgical resection was lobectomy in 56, pneumonectomy in 33, bilobectomy in 6, and limited resection in 5 patients. Patients with R1 resections received radiotherapy additionally to adjuvant chemotherapy. Patient characteristics are listed in table 1. Postoperative recurrence was seen in 46 patients (17 local, 17 distant, 12 both local and distant). Overall, 50% of patients were deceased. Recurrence free survival time was significantly different between deceased and survived patients (14.8 ±2.5 versus 17.7±5.12 months; p=0.020). Median overall survival was 26 months (95%CI: 15.11-36.88). ECOG performance status was the only factor significantly associated with survival (95%CI: 1.07-5.74, p=0.034). Conclusion: In this retrospective cohort of patients with resected early stage lung cancer, ECOG performance status was the only factor significantly associated with survival. We could not find a significant association between 8th TNM stage descriptors, tumor histology, and presence of certain tumor characteristics such as visceral pleural, perineural and vascular invasion. (Table pressented).
