N2  - Objective: Lobectomy is the gold standard for the treatment of early-stage non-small-cell lung cancer. Anatomical segmentectomy is advocated for curative resection in select patients. We investigated the safety and oncological efficacy of robot-assisted anatomical segmentectomy with mediastinal nodal dissection. Methods: We retrospectively reviewed patients who underwent robotassisted anatomical segmentectomy for early-stage non-small-cell lung cancer. Robotic dissection of the main bronchovascular structures was followed by division of the respective segmental structures and mediational nodal dissection. Follow-up data were obtained to determine the survival rate, along with statistically significant risk factors in both univariate and multivariate models. Results: A total of 153 patients underwent robot-assisted anatomical segmentectomy. Selection criteria included poor pulmonary function and advanced age. There were 78 men, 75 women; the mean age was 70 ± 12 years. All patients underwent R0 resection. The mean operating time was 134 minutes; 24/153 patients (16%) were upstaged. Another 19/24 patients were upstaged due to the size of the tumor in the pathological specimen (>3 cm), and 5/24 patients were upstaged due to microscopic nodalmetastases. Themedian hospitalization was 7 days (2-31 d). The complication rate was 29%. There were no deaths. The mean followup time was 30 months (range, 2 months-9 years) in 145/153 (95%) patients. The overall 5-year survival ratewas 43%, whereas the lung cancerspecific 5-year survival rate was 55%. The 5-year lung cancer-specific survival rate for pathological stage I disease was 73%. Local or mediastinal recurrence occurred in 7/145 (5%) patients. Pathological upstaging or recurrence resulted in a 0% 5-year survival rate. Univariate and multivariate analysis showed that advanced age and pathological upstaging were statistically significant risk factors for lung cancer-specific death. Conclusions: Robotic segmentectomy with mediastinal nodal dissection is a safe and oncologically efficacious procedure. Accurate preoperative clinical staging is of critical importance in long-term survival.
