N2  - Background: The goal of the present study is to estimate the rate of local/regional failure (LRF) following definitive surgery for early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) (without postoperative radiation) in the era of contemporary imaging and surgical techniques such as PET/CT and video assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS). Methods: After IRB approval, we retrospectively reviewed the medical records of patients with early stage NSCLC (pathologic T1-4, N0-1) who underwent anatomical resection (e.g. lobectomy, sleeve lobectomy, bilobectomy, or pneumonectomy) with/without adjuvant chemotherapy between 2007 and 2015. Data recorded included demographics, preoperative imaging and staging procedures, surgical technique, histology, pathologic stage, risk factors for recurrence (e.g. margins, invasion), adjuvant chemotherapy, site of LRF, and survival. LRF was defined as recurrence at the ipsilateral lung, bronchial stump, mediastinum, chest wall, or supraclavicular region. The Kaplan Meier method was used to compare time to LRF and estimate rates of 5 year LRF; the Log-rank test was used for comparisons and patients were censored at death. Results: A total of 217 patients were included, with a median age of 65 years. Preoperative staging via PET/CT was done in 89% of patients; mediastinoscopy was done in 42% of patients. Surgical procedure was VATS in 50% of the patients. At a median follow up of 3 years (range 1 month - 10 years), the 5 year estimated LRF was 26% (95% CI: 20%-35%). Estimated 5 year LRF rates were significantly higher in males compared to females (34% vs 17%, p=0.03), those with lymphovascular space invasion (38% vs 25% p=0.03), and disease stage II-III (≥T2b or N1-2) compared to stage I (T1-2a, N0) (42% vs 21%, p=0.01). LRF rates in those with and without staging PET/CT was not significantly different (26% vs 21%, p=0.42). Conclusion: Despite the use of contemporary staging procedures and surgical techniques for early stage NSCLC, about one out of four patients experience LRF. And the observed rates of LRF are similar to what has been reported in previous years (1996-2006) suggesting local/regional control remains a persistent problem. The use of additional local treatments such as radiation should be re-evaluated to further improve outcomes.
