N2  - Purpose/Objective(s): Previous efforts to identify factors associated with treatment selection of surgery versus stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) in clinical stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients have used retrospective data, which can be limited by the ability to obtain all desired patient and tumor characteristics. Our goal was to design and implement a prospective database of clinical Stage 1 NSCLC patients receiving either surgery or SBRT at a single, high-volume institution. Purpose/Objective(s): A prospective REDCap database has been created to capture comprehensive data elements for stage I NSCLC patients receiving either pulmonary resection or SBRT. Patients are recruited for inclusion into this database in both our radiation oncology and thoracic surgery clinics. Following informed consent, clinical data regarding the patient's tumor, imaging, and laboratory values are collected, as well as additional questionnaires targeted at comorbidity profiles, functional status, and baseline quality-of-life (QoL) metrics. Follow-up will include both short-and long-term survival and QoL outcomes. Results: Over a period of nine months, 957 clinic patients have been screened to date. 83 patients (8.7%) consented to the study, representing 42% of the accrual goal (83/200). 12 patients have withdrawn (15.5%). Of the 71 patients currently enrolled, 34 have received surgery (47.9%), 34 received SBRT (47.9%), and 3 (4.2%) are awaiting treatment. Demographics at this time show the mean age of study patients is 66 years (SD 9.1), 59% female, and 85% Caucasian. Current analysis shows that patients receiving SBRT are older (69.8 years vs 62.7, p=0.06) and have a worse cardiopulmonary status [FEV1percent predicted 64.2% vs 84.8%, p<0.001, arterial partial pressure of oxygen (PaO2), 76.7 vs 82.2, p=0.03]. A larger proportion of surgical patients have a higher functional status (Zubrod 0, 80.0% vs. 33.3%, p=0.01). Conclusion: Early analysis demonstrates that clinical stage I NSCLC patients have a poorer physiologic profile and functional status compared to surgical patients. Accrual to this prospective multi-disciplinary database will facilitate the identification of granular clinical factors to predict high-risk operative stage I NSCLC patients that may be more appropriate for SBRT therapy. Outcomes and quality of life data collected during follow-up will also assist with the shared decision making process.
