N2  - Objectives: The surgical intervention for small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) is recommended only for early stage diseases, but this recommendation is based on low-level evidence. Especially for locally advanced disease (nodal positive limited disease), the surgical indication is controversial. Since the prognosis of non-small cell lung carcinoma has largely improved by the development of molecular target drug and immunological agent, the therapeutic strategy for SCLC must also be discussed to move on to the next stage. Methods: We retrospectively investigated 56 surgical cases of SCLC treated at our institution from 2005 to 2016. Survival rate was evaluated with the Kaplan-Meier method, and Log-rank test was used to compare the different arms. Results: There were 51 males, and 5 females. Average age was 69.3±8.0 years. Forty-one cases were pathologically diagnosed as pure-SCLC, whereas 15 cases were diagnosed as combined SCLC. Lobectomy was the procedure most frequently performed (n = 47), followed by bilobectomy (n = 4), segmentectomy (n = 4), and pneumonectomy (n = 1). Mediastinal lymph node dissection was performed in 35 cases. Pathological stage were IA (n=13), IB (n = 12), IIA (n=13), IIB (n = 3), IIIA (n=14), IIIB (n = 1), respectively. R0 resection was completed in 51 cases. Median observation period was 27 months. Overall survival (OS) and disease free survival (DFS) at 2 years were 75% and 62%, and OS and DFS at 5 years were 32% and 35%, respectively. As for advanced stage SCLC (p-stageIIA-IIIB), OS and DFS at 2 and 5 years were 70%, 57% and 32%, 35% each. There were 5 longterm survivors without recurrence or metastasis for over than 4 years in spite of the advanced stage. There were no significant differences between pure SCLC and combined SCLC (OS at 2 years: 70.3% vs 70.3%, P=0.93). Conclusions: There were some patients who benefit from surgery even in the advanced stage SCLC. Further discussion is needed to verify the potential of surgical intervention for SCLC.
