N2  - Objectives: Although VATS left upper division (VATS LUD) resection preserves more lung parenchyma than VATS left upper lobe (VATS LUL) lobectomy, potential benefits of preserving pulmonary function are unknown. This study aims to compare postoperative pulmonary function after VATS LUD resection to those after VATS LUL lobectomy. Methods: This is a retrospective cohort study with 30 clinical stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients with both pre-and post-pulmonary function test from June 2013 to February 2016 by a single surgeon at a university hospital in Seoul, Korea. Preoperative FVC(L), FVC (%), FEV1(L), FEV1(%), FEV1/FVC (%), DLCO, and DLCO (%) were compared to those at 6 months after surgery using Wilcoxon signed rank test and Fisher's exact test. Results: There were 16 and 14 patients undergoing VATS LUL lobectomy and VATS left upper lobe division resection, respectively. Number of mediastinal lymph nodes (MLND), operative time, pulmonary complications, and length of hospital stay were not different between the groups. The preoperative baseline pulmonary function of both groups was comparable. A patient who had undergone VATS LUD resection (-12.54L) had slightly more reduced FVC than patients with VATS LUL lobectomy FVC (-10.17L) at 6 months after operation (P=0.459). There was minimal advantage in FEV1/FVC (%) in cases of VATS LUD resection than VATS LUL lobectomy although this was statically non-significant (P=0.0871). There was no significant difference in postoperative DLCO between the groups. Conclusions: Although VATS LUD resection anatomically reduces the extent of lung resection in comparison to VATS LUL lobectomy, there were no benefits of pulmonary function. Therefore, the decision to perform VATS LUD resection or VATS LUL lobectomy is left to the availability of the expertise and the feasibility of the procedure.
