N2  - RATIONALE Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is a known common type of pulmonary malignancy with epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) mutation prevalence of 30-50% found in Asian population. The evaluation of EGFR genes were previously detected only from tumor tissue samples which have obvious restrictions. Surgical intervention can be accomplished in early stages while late stages can be treated by tyrosine-kinase inhibitors (TKIs) which have been proven effective with lesser treatment-related complications. Plasma EGFR or liquid biopsy is a non-invasive test that detects EGFR mutation faster than tissue biopsy samples. METHODS Plasma samples were collected on eight patients diagnosed of adenocarcinoma with EGFR mutations positive from tissue specimen. Four were in early stages and remaining four in late stages. For early stages, one was screened for circulating free DNA (cfDNA) to detect EGFR mutation prior and post-surgery. The other three participants in early stages had their cfDNA measured only after surgery. For four subjects in late stages, three had their serum samples collected after long term TKI therapy and one had her plasma examined prior treatment. RESULTS The four participants in early stages of lung cancer all underwent surgery. Plasma before and four days post lobectomy was collected on the first patient. Prior surgery result was positive of EGFR mutation and post-surgery result was negative. The three remaining participants had their plasma collected after surgery which revealed negative results. The samples were gathered between four and ninety days post-operatively. For late stages, one patient had plasma collected at the same time when tissue biopsy was performed. Result was positive of EGFR mutation from plasma and positive of adenocarcinoma from the tissue specimen. TKI therapy was started before biomarkers from tissue samples were released. For the remaining three participants, liquid biopsy was performed after TKI therapy and EGFR exon 20 T790M mutation was found on one subject, showing resistance. CONCLUSION Plasma EGFR is an important breakthrough that can detect EGFR gene mutation in a non-invasive technique which can be performed at any stage of lung cancer. This test can screen early stage of malignancy, compare tissue and blood test results, monitor presence of mutation after surgery, recognize occurrence of TKI resistance and help avoid unnecessary invasive procedures. In our study, although in a very small scale, has proven that this screening method provides information that could help patient and physician especially among Asian population in making a prompt and sound decision.
