N2  - Rationale: Several studies have shown that Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) characterised by airflow limitation is a significant independent risk factor for lung cancer, affecting between 50-60% of lung cancer patients. In a post-hoc analysis of the NLST-ACRIN study, we have shown that COPD is associated with a 2-fold increase in all-cause mortality. We conducted a systematic review to answer the question “In non-small cell lung cancer does pre-existing COPD lead to worse survival outcomes after lung cancer surgery?” Aim: The primary aim of this study was to deduce from a systematic review whether the presence of COPD had a significant impact on the 5-year survival of patients who underwent lung cancer surgery for early stage NSCLC. The secondary aim of the study was to confirm our findings from the systematic review in a post-hoc analysis of the NLST-ACRIN study (N=10,054). Methods: A systematic literature search was performed in OVID, MEDLINE, EMBASE, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews and Google Scholar with no language restriction published, between January 1946 to January 2016. The abstracts were selected from keywords including “non-small cell lung cancer/lung cancer/adenocarcinoma”, “pneumonectomy/lobectomy/resection/removal” and “mortality/survival” and according to an eligibility score, the full-text article was fully analysed. Eligibility for further analysis was checked by two reviewers and a third reviewer resolved any discrepancies. Data extraction and analysis was performed using a standardised record form and method (PRISMA). From 83 full articles we identified 11 studies that fitted the stringent screening process for inclusion into the review. Results: From these 11 studies, 7,588 subjects were included in the systematic review. Of these 11 studies, 8 were retrospective, 3 were prospective and there were no randomised controlled trials. There were no signs of heterogeneity or publication bias as assessed by Begg's funnel plot and Egger test (heterogeneity testing p = 0.504; I2 = 0.0%). The overall hazard ratio for all-cause mortality comparing those with and without COPD was 0.96 (95% CI =0.91-1.02)(Figure 1). In the NLST-ACRIN screening sub-study we found a comparable result in a screened population. Conclusions: In our systematic review of unscreened lung cancer cases, there was no significant difference in 5-year survival for those with and without COPD following lung cancer surgery for early stage NSCLC. This finding was confirmed in our NLST screening sub-study. Figure 1. Forest plot of 5 year overall survival odds ratios for the 11 studies in this systematic review.
