N2  - Currently, lung cancer remains the most common cause of cancer-related death in the United States and worldwide. Overall five-year survival rates for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) vary from 4 to 17 percent. Surgery is the standard treatment for patients with clinical stage I and II NSCLC, in whom there is no evidence of mediastinal involvement. We present a case where a patient beat the five-year survival odds after choosing not to pursue lobectomy, which based on expert opinion, was standard of treatment. The patient, however, returned nine years later with progression of disease. A 67 year-old female with essential hypertension, multi-nodular goiter, and hyperlipidemia was evaluated in 2009 for a right upper lobe nodule found accidently on chest x-ray. The nodule was further evaluated by computed tomography (CT), which revealed it was 1.5 by 1.6 centimeters (cm) in size. Ultimately she was diagnosed with bronchoalveolar carcinoma in situ, however chose not to pursue recommended lobectomy, but opted for surveillance. The patient was lost to follow-up for nine years, however returned after a CT of the chest was obtained for cough and fever, which revealed growth in size of the mass to 4.2 by 3.7 cm with innumerable pulmonary nodules. Based on cytology she was stage IV T2a N1 M0. The majority of data relating to prognosis based on staging comes from a series of over 31,000 cases from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results database. In that series, survival progressively decreased with increasing clinical stage, with five-year survival rates for stages IA, IB, IIA, and IIB of 50, 43, 36, and 25 percent, respectively. Although this patient survived without treatment nine years post diagnosis, this is unusual. This highlights the importance to assess the balance between morbidity associated with treatment versus without treatment in patients with advanced age. Diagnosis of an early stage bronchoalveolar carcinoma in situ may not need treatment to obtain a high five-year survival rate. Nevertheless, primary screening for certain high-risk individuals should occur. Earlier detection of lung cancer may lead to earlier assessment for intervention.
