N2  - Introduction: Pulmonary artery pseudoaneurysm (PAP) is uncommonly seen in lung cancer and is rarely reported as a complication of stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) for early non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) . PAP can be potentially fatal and therefore hightened awareness by clinicians to this rare entity is essential. Case: A 70 year old female with a medical history of oxygen dependent chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and stage 1A right upper lobe lung adenocarcinoma treated with right upper lobectomy in 1992. She had a surveillance computed tomography (CT) of the chest done almost 2 decades later which demonstrated a new 8mm nodule in the right lower lobe which was metabolically active with a maximum SUV of 6.9. Repeat CT chest done one month later revealed an increase in the size of the nodule to 11mm. Quantitative ventilation/perfusion scan demonstrated 52% perfusion to the left and 48% perfusion to the right lung. Surgical intervention was not deemed appropriate and biopsy was not thought to be possible. But given suspicion of primary malignancy, she underwent SBRT at a dose of 54 Gy in 3 fractions. She obtained a complete response without any evidence of recurrence in follow-up imaging studies. However, she suffered massive hemoptysis 2 years after SBRT necessitating intubation. Localized area of fibrosis was observed in the right lower lobe in accordance with SBRT field on the CT chest. Pulmonary arteriography revealed a wide mouth pulmonary artery pseudoaneurysm (PAP) emanating from the right inter lobar artery for which she underwent pulmonary artery embolisation. She had another episode of hemoptysis 2 days later, repeat arteriogram revealed appropriately coiled PAP but stent graft was deployed across the mouth of the PAP. Unfortunately, patient opted for palliative measures in event of repeat hemoptysis and was therefore discharged home on comfort measures. Discussion: SBRT has become standard of care for early stage NSCLC with excellent clinical outcomes in medically inoperable patients. Although SBRT is usually safe with acceptable toxicity profile, but centrally located tumors are at high-risk location with close proximity to radiation-sensitive structures such as heart, great vessels and large airways and therefore can predispose the patients to unique toxicity profile. PAP is a serious condition and can present with life threatening hemoptysis. Early identification and treatment is essential to prevent rupture, hemorrhage and death. Treatment varies from embolisation to surgery, but should be tailored to the patient's clinical presentation. (Figure presented).
