N2  - Introduction Non-small cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC) may present with concurrent mediastinal and/or hilar lymphadenopathy on imaging. Because of prognostic and therapeutic implications, it is crucial to distinguish metastatic lymphadenopathy from benign causes. Case presentation A 78-year-old male with a 40-pack year smoking history was found to have an incidental nodule in the right upper lobe on Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) of the spine, done following a fall. Subsequent Computerized Tomography (CT) of the chest confirmed a 1.8 cm irregular nodular density. Bilateral mediastinal and left hilar lymphadenopathy (Figure, green arrows) was noted. A CT guided biopsy confirmed the mass as a moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma and the patient underwent a Positron Emission Tomography (PET) scan. The nodular lesion in the right lung apex was found to be fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)-avid. Mediastinal and hilar lymph nodes and a right pleural nodule were also FDG-avid. The patient underwent a right medical thoracoscopy with pleural biopsy which was benign and then endobronchial ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) of mediastinal and hilar lymph nodes which were also benign. He then had a right upper lobectomy along with mediastinal lymph node dissection. Pathological examination of parabronchial and subcarinal lymph nodes showed caseating granulomas. Other lymph nodes were negative for malignancy. Thus in the absence of metastases to the lymph nodes, the patient's cancer was staged as stage I NSCLC with no further adjuvant therapy necessary. Discussion Reactive granulomatous inflammation of the lymph nodes associated with malignancy otherwise also known as sarcoid-like reaction is occasionally seen in patients with NSCLC. It is estimated to occur in about 1-4% of patients with NSCLC. It is benign and is postulated to be a hypersensitivity reaction to the tumor antigens presenting to the lymph nodes draining the primary cancer with some reports suggesting it implies a stronger immune system and perhaps a better prognosis. Non-caseating granulomas (unlike our case) are more common. Other causes of granulomatous inflammation such as tuberculosis, sarcoidosis and fungal infections should be excluded before making the diagnosis. Sarcoid like reaction can sometimes be difficult to distinguish from malignancy on PET scan. Hence it is imperative that patients with FDG-avid mediastinal and/or hilar lymphadenopathy undergo pathological examination following either mediastinoscopy and dissection or EBUS-TBNA. This allows for proper staging of the cancer and may save the patient from harmful effects of systemic therapy. Thus in our patient surgical excision of the tumor would be considered as curative. (Figure presented).
