N2  - Background: Post-operative UFT (tegafur/uracil) has been shown to prolong survival of Japanese pts with completely resected, p-stage I (T1 > 2 cm) NSCLC. This trial aimed at estimating the efficacy of TS1 (tegafur/gimeracil/oteracil) compared to UFT as adjuvant therapy in this population. Methods: Eligible pts had received complete resection with lymph node dissection for p-stage I (T1-2N0M0, T1 > 2 cm, by 5t h Edition UICC TNM) NSCLC, within 56 days of enrollment. Pts were randomized to receive: oral UFT 250mg/M2/d for 2 years (Arm A), or oral TS1 80mg/M2/d for 2 weeks and 1 week rest, for 1 year (Arm B). The initial primary endpoint was overall survival (OS). Based upon the monitoring in Jun. 2013, which showed the combined OS of the 2 arms better than expected (4-year OS of 91.6% vs. presumed 5-year OS of 70-76.5%), it was judged to be underpowered. The study protocol was amended so that the primary endpoint is relapse-free survival (RFS). With the calculated sample size of 960, this study would detect the superiority of Arm B over Arm A with power 79% and one-sided type I error of 0.05, assuming the 5-year RFS of 75% in Arm A and the hazard ratio of 0.75. Results: From Nov. 2008 to Dec. 2013, 963 pts were enrolled: median age 66 (range: 33 to 80), male 58%, adenocarcinoma 80%, p-T1/T2 46%/54%. Only 2 received pneumonectomy. At the data cut-off in May 2015, 94% of pts in Arm A and all in Arm B completed protocol therapy. ≥. Grade 3 toxicities (hematologic/nonhematologic) were observed in 15.7 (1.5/14.5) % in Arm A, and in 14.4 (3.4/11.9) % in Arm B, respectively. 65% of the pts in Arm A received UFT for .≥ 1 year, whereas 69% of Arm B received TS1 for .≥ 6 months. There were 4 cases of on-protocol deaths, probably of cardio-vascular origin, 1 in Arm A and 3 in Arm B. Based on the 2n d interim analysis in Sep. 2015, the data and safety monitoring committee recommended follow-up of pts without unmasking of treatment arms. Estimated combined 2-year OS and RFS were 97.3% and 89.6%, respectively. Conclusions: Both post-operative adjuvant therapies were feasible, with similar compliances. Main results will be available in 2019.
