N2  - RATIONALE: Thoracoscopic surgery for lung cancer patients is widely being performed with a growth of social expectations for low-invasive operation. However, this technically demanding procedure, which requires a second learning curve even for skilled specialists, seems to be insecure especially for trainees considering many catastrophic intraoperative complications reported to date in Japan. To ensure the safety of lung cancer operation for trainees, we have developed a minimally invasive “hybrid” video-assisted surgical approach which affords hilar control with direct-view manipulation. The aim of present study was to verify the safety and low invasiveness of our hybrid video-assisted surgery (HVAS) for lung cancer by evaluation of its perioperative outcomes. METHODS: From January 2008 to December 2013, a total of 246 consecutive non-randomized patients with clinical stage I non-small-cell lung cancer underwent lobectomy by HVAS with systematic mediastinal lymph node dissection. The patients were divided into two groups: the early period group (From January 2008 to December 2010, n=116), and the late period group (From January 2011 to December 2013, n=130). There were no significant differences between the two surgical groups with respect to age, preoperative respiratory functional data, tumor diameter, the procedure performed or pathological diagnosis. In both groups, an access hole about 6cm in length was made in the 5th intercostal space under the auscultatory triangle with two multipurpose thoracoports. The HVAS procedure was performed with occasional direct-view manipulation through the access hole as necessary. Data were collected retrospectively. RESULTS: Conversion rate from HVAS procedure to open thoracotomy was 0% in both groups. The mean operative time was 248 minutes in the early period group and 132 minutes in the late period group (p<0.0001). The mean blood loss was 128 ml in the early period group and 26 ml in the late period group (p<0.0001). No significant differences were seen between the two groups with respect to the total number of dissected mediastinal lymph nodes. Mortality was 0% in both groups. In-hospital morbidity was 18.1% (21/116) in the early period group and 9.2% (12/130) in the late period group, respectively (p<0.05). CONCLUSIONS: Perioperative outcomes of our HVAS technique for lung cancer were remarkably being improved by accumulated experiences in the continuous series of operations. We assume that the safety and minimal invasiveness of our latest HVAS method were shown by the results of this study. Long-term disease-free and overall survival data are needed to evaluate our oncologic efficacy with this technique.
