N2  - Rationale: Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) survivors are at increased risk of for a second primary lung cancer. In this study, we compared treatment strategies for early stage second primary lung cancer using population-based cancer data. Methods: We used the Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results registry linked to Medicare claims to identify resected patients ≥65 years of age with histologically confirmed stage I-II NSCLC diagnosed between 1992-2009. Among these, we selected cases diagnosed with a second primary NSCLC during the follow-up period. We used propensity score methods to compare survival of patients with early stage second primaries that underwent: 1) radiotherapy (RT) alone vs. no treatment or 2) sub-lobar (wedge or segmentectomy) vs. full (lobectomy/pneumonectomy) resection. Results: We identified 1,062 patients with stage I-II NSCLC who had a second lung primary after curative resection for a first NSCLC. Mean age at second diagnosis was 75 years, 52% were female and 91% where white. Tumors were most frequently of early stage (77% I/II) and most often were adenocarcinomas (60%). Median survival was 28 months (IQR: 18-44 months) vs. 20 months (IQR 15-33 months) for untreated patients vs. those who underwent RT, respectively (p=0.57). Median survival following sublobar (48 months; IQR 42-55 months) vs. full (49 months; IQR 43-59 months) resection was also similar (p=0.77). Adjusted analyses also showed no significant benefit with RT alone (HR: hazard ratio [HR]: 0.79; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.49-1.28) vs. no treatment. In patients who underwent sublobar vs. full resection there was also no difference in survival (HR: 1.03; 95% CI: 0.84-1.26) after adjusting for propensity scores. Conclusion: A large proportion of secondary primaries are early potentially curable NSCLC. Sublobar resection appears to be equivalent to full resection for older patients with secondary NSCLCs. However, RT alone may not improve survival. Older patients with early stage secondary primary NSCLC can achieve relatively good long-term survival if they are candidates for resection.
