N2  - Objectives: Completion pneumonectomy (CP) is a major procedure known to have a high morbidity and mortality, but often it offers the last chance to cure the patient. The aim of our study is to evaluate the factors influencing postoperative and long-term survival after completion pneumonectomy. Methods: We retrospectively reviewed the records of 99 patients who underwent a completion pneumonectomy in the thoracic surgery department of the European Georges Pompidou Hospital and of the University Hospital of Amiens between 1980 and 2013. Results: Surgery was performed on the right side for 49 patients. The indication for the first surgery was benign diseases (BD) in 11 cases and malignant disease (MD) in 88 cases. The average time between the first surgery and CP was 72.9 months. There were 76 CP for MD, including 70 NSCLCs, and 23 for BD, including 8 emergency operations. Global morbidity was 51.5% (46% for the MD and 69.6% for the BD, P = 0.021). There were 22 reoperations (17.1% in the MD group and 39.1% in the BD group, P = 0.033) with significantly more reoperations on the right side (30.6% vs 14%, P = 0.038). 77.8% of fistulas occurred after CP on the right side (P = 0.091). Mortality at 90 days was 17.2% (13.2% for the MD and 30.4% for the BD, P = 0.028). For the CP performed in an emergency setting, postoperative mortality was 50% (P = 0.089). The overall actuarial 5-year survival was 31.3%. The long-term survival increased significantly with the interval between the first surgery and the CP (P = 0.025). Cancer stage also seemed to influence long-term survival (Stage I vs Stage III; P = 0.055). Conclusions: Completion pneumonectomy for benign disease is a risky intervention, especially when performed in an emergency setting. Morbidity and mortality after CP for cancer remains acceptable, but long-term results are better if the stage is lower and the interval between surgeries longer.
