N2  - Objective: It is not clear whether VATS lobectomy is beneficial in patients with poor pulmonary lung function. The aim of our study was to evaluate the postoperative outcome and long-term outcomes of patients with poor lung function who underwent thoracoscopic lobectomy for pathologic stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). Methods: The patients who underwent lobectomy by either thoracotomy or VATS for pathologic stage I NSCLC were retrospectively reviewed. The predicted postoperative (ppo) parameters of forced expiratory volume in 1 second (ppoFEV1) and diffusing capacity of the lung for carbon monoxide (ppoDLCO) were calculated for patients who underwent lobectomy. We divided the patients depending on ppoFEV1% (group 1: 80%) and ppoDLCO% (group A: 80%). Data were analyzed with respect to demographics, risk factors, and early postoperative outcome and survival according to ppo- FEV1% and ppoDLCO%. Results: From 2007 to 2008, 244 patients with pathologic stage I NSCLC underwent lobectomy (open, n=62; VATS, n=182). The median follow-up duration period was 53.3months (range, 1-102). The overall survival rate at 5 years was 80.6%. The perioperative mortality rate (within 60 days from surgery) was 6.5% (4/62) in the open group and 2.2% (4/182) in the VATS group. The overall morbidity rate was 27.4 % (17/62) in the open group and 25.8% (47/182) in the VATS group (P=0.879). The rate of cardiopulmonary complications was 19.4% (12/62) in the open group and 16.5% (30/182) in the VATS group. With decreasing ppoFEV1%, the cardiopulmonary complication increased at a greater rate in the open group than in the VATS group (Fig. P67-1). However, there was no relationship between ppoDLCO% and cardiopulmonary complications. Conclusions: Video-assisted thoracic lobectomy for cancer in patients with poor lung function can achieve acceptable postoperative outcome and oncologic outcome compared with open thoracotomy. (Figure Presented).
