N2  - Introduction: Adjuvant platinum based chemotherapy for Stage II III resected non-small cell lung cancer is considered standard of care. Cisplatin vinorelbine has a robust evidence base to support it, however carboplatin based treatment is used in the UK in patients with borderline performance status. This study aims to compare outcomes in a clinically relevant population. Methods: A retrospective audit of 177 patients who underwent adjuvant chemotherapy for Non small cell lung cancer in Merseyside from 2008 to 2013 was carried out. Data on surgery, performance status, stage, chemotherapy regime and outcomes was gathered from the patient records and analysed. Results: Interim results (from 89 patient records analysed so far) from a group of 89 patients with a median age of 66.5 yrs and a median follow up of 22 months are shown. 72% were PS 0 to 1. 71% had a lobectomy, 27% pneumonectomy, there was a median delay of 58 days from surgery to start of chemotherapy. 58% were Stage I to II, 42% Stage 3A and above. 59% of the patients had cisplatin based chemotherapy, 41% carboplatin based. 53% completed all planned treatment (74% of carboplatin patients and 38% of cisplatin patients); 47% did not complete planned treatment, 72% of these permanently stopped treatment. OS at 6, 12, 24 and 36 months was 98.8%, 93.5%, 81% and 69% respectively with an estimated median survival of 52 months. There was no statistically significant difference in OS for cisplatin, 54.7 months (47-62 months), compared to Carboplatin, 46.7 months (35-58 months) (p value 0.325). Conclusion: Carboplatin based Adjuvant NSCLung cancer chemotherapy does not appear to have significantly different survival outcomes from cisplatin based treatment in a clinically relevant patient group. (Figure Presented).
