N2  - Introduction: For patients with resectable non small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) NICE recommends surgical resection as gold standard radical treatment. The LACE meta-analysis suggested that adjuvant Cisplatin-based chemotherapy has an additional beneficial effect on survival. We reviewed current practice to investigate the management and outcomes of this patient group. Methods: A retrospective analysis was undertaken of all patients who received surgery for NSCLC in South East Wales between 2010 and 2013. Data was collated from the Velindre Cancer Centre electronic notes system and LUCADA datasets. Results: 111 patients were identified from 6 South East Wales hospitals. Median age 68 years old, all had a pre-operative WHO performance status ≤ 2 (91.9% PS0/1, 8.1% PS2). The TNM staging: 4.5% IA, 18.9% IB, 28.8% IIA, 21.6% IIB, 24.3% IIIA, 1.8% IIIB, 0% IV. 91.9% of patients underwent a lobectomy, whilst 6.3% had a pneumonectomy and 1.8% had a wedge resection. Resections were classed as R0 in 87.5%, and R1 in 12.5%. Adjuvant radiotherapy was given in 11.7%. 64.9% of patients received adjuvant chemotherapy (of those 84.7% received a Cisplatin doublet, the rest carboplatin). No deaths were identified within 30 days of chemotherapy. Of those who did not receive chemotherapy, 76.9% had a poor performance status or significant co-morbidities and 23.1% declined treatment. The three year overall survival (OS) rate for all patients was 48.6% (three year OS rate for stage I, II and III disease of 56.6%, 47.8% and 42.2% respectively). Conclusion: Appropriate use of adjuvant chemotherapy was observed with a high rate of compliance with Cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy and low morbidity. The observed survival rates are broadly comparable with those seen in the LACE meta-analysis allowing for the unselected patient population.
