N2  - Introduction: Epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) (Gefitinib®, Erlotinib®) are effectively used as first line treatment in stage III and IV Non-Squamous NSCLC (NSNSCLC) harbouring the EGFR gene mutation and as second or third line therapy in patients with advanced NSCLC who have failed to respond to conventional chemotherapy. Here we present a case of complete pathological response following Gefitinib® neo-adjuvant treatment in initial Stage IIIA mutated adenocarcinoma of the lung. Methods: We report herein a case of a 59 yrs old lady with a stage IIIA left upper lobe EGFR mutated primary lung adenocarcinoma (T1aN2M0) and previous breast cancer treated with surgical excision and adjuvant chemo-radio-therapy. Results: The patient underwent a standard Cisplatin® + Vinorelbine® protocol with stable disease after 3 cycles. This was followed by a regimen of 250 mg daily administration of Gefitinib® for 4 months. A drastic reduction of the mediastinal tumour burden (T1aN0) was found radiologically and confirmed following invasive (EBUS and subsequent mediastinoscopy) re-staging. A left upper lobectomy with mediastinal nodal dissection was then performed. The pathological report showed a complete response with absence of tumour in the specimen (pT0N0M0) (Figure 1). CT-scan follow-up at 6 months showed no evidence of tumour recurrence. Conclusion: As well as in the present case, EGFR-TKIs are a valid option to treat advanced NSCLC. Little evidence is currently available regarding the use of EGFR-TKIs in locally advanced NSCLC harbouring EGFR mutations. Our report raises multiple questions such as: a) the role of EGFR-TKIs as neo-adjuvant agent in the treatment of locally advanced as well as early stage mutated NSNSCLC; b) the appropriate non-invasive and invasive algorithm of restaging after induction treatment; c) the role and the appropriate timing of surgery after complete response to induction treatment (T0N0M0). We believe that answers could be provided by further prospective and multicentre studies. (Figure Presented).
