N2  - Purpose/Objective(s): The role of adjuvant chemotherapy for early stage NSCLC stage I patients with tumors size ≥ 4 cm is not well established. Secondary analysis of CALGB 9633, the only randomized clinical trial designed for these cancers did show a potential benefit but these findings have not been validated in the elderly. Materials/Methods: We identified 2923 patients with stage I NSCLC who underwent lobectomy from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER)-Medicare linked database diagnosed from 1992 to 2009. Overall survival and rates of serious adverse events (defined as those requiring admission to hospital) were compared between patients treated with resection alone, platinum-based adjuvant chemotherapy, or adjuvant chemotherapy and radiation. Propensity scores for receiving each treatment were calculated using a logistic model that included socio-demographic characteristics, comorbidities, and cancer-related factors. Survival analyses were conducted with a Cox regression model using inverse probability weights based on the estimated propensity score. Results: Overall, 88.6% patients were treated with resection alone, 10.3% received platinum-based adjuvant chemotherapy, and 1.0% underwent chemotherapy and radiation. Adjusted analysis showed that adjuvant chemotherapy (hazard ratio [HR], 0.82; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.68-0.99) was associated with improved survival compared to resection alone. Conversely, adjuvant chemotherapy and radiation (HR 2.10; 95% CI, 1.40-3.17) were associated with worse outcomes. Patients receiving adjuvant chemotherapy had more serious adverse events compared to those treated with resection alone, with neutropenia being most significant (odds ratio, 11.3; 95% CI, 3.6-36.0). No significant difference was observed in rates of fever, anemia, thrombocytopenia, nausea, and acute renal failure. Conclusions: Platinum-based adjuvant chemotherapy is associated with reduced mortality and increased serious adverse events in elderly patients with stage I NSCLC and tumor size ≥ 4 cm. Our findings are similar to that observed in CALGB 9633, thereby supporting the consideration of adjuvant chemotherapy in this patient group.
