N2  - Introduction: There is growing evidence that RFA-mediated necrosis can modulate host immune responses. In patients with early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCL) we applied radiofrequency ablation (RFA), followed by tumor resection. In this regimen RFA may give rise to necrotic tumor fragments in vivo, which may booster immunity by endogenous DC initiating an in-situ immunization against tumor antigenes. Methods: In a total of 4 patients with solitary pulmonary lesions CT guided biopsy proofed NSCLC and RFA was performed in the same intervention. Radical tumor resection was performed (by VATS-lobectomy, mediastinal and hilar lymph node dissection) 10 days subsequent to RFA. To investigate the role of DC, four blood samples were collected (before RFA, 3 days, 2, 4 weeks subsequent to RFA). DC populations were isolated by magnetic beads and analyzed by flow cytometry. To address the functional changes in DC, cocultures of allogeneic CD4+ T cell with DC were set up and the proliferation was measured 3 days thereafter. Finally we investigated the production of cytokines by DC. Results: The number of CD11c+CD14-CD19- DC significantly increased over the 4 week observation period after RFA. The further dissection of the DC into mDC and pDC subpopulations, respectively, showed that this increase was due to elevated numbers of BDCA-1+ /BDAC-2+ mDC. In contrast the number of BDCA2+ pDC remained unchanged. When analyzing the activation status of the DC it became apparent that after RFA expression of CD83 by DC was upregulated, indicating a maturation of DC. Moreover, the inhibitory molecules B7H2 and B7H3 were clearly down regulated after RFA and remained low, during the observation period. These data indicate that RFA treatment induces a rather mature immunostimulatory phenotype of DC. In the mixed leuocyte reactions we detected enhanced allostimulatory capacity of the DC after RFA, which was apparent during the observation period. When similar co-culture experiments were set up with syngeneic CD8+ T cells, increased proliferation subsequent to RFA was also apparent. Investigating the production of cytokines by DC elevated levels of IFN γ were detected in DC stimulated with TLR ligands such as LPS and PolyI:C. Conclusion: For the first time we could show that RFA of early stage NSCLC results in elevated numbers of activated DC in peripheral blood. Tumor ablation augments the anti-tumor immune responses in vivo and potentially may lead to an in-situ immunization against tumor antigens in this hybrid concept including RFA and subsequent surgical tumor resection.
