N2  - Background: The standard treatment of early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is lobectomy. However, a considerable number of older patients are not candidates for lobectomy due to comorbidities or limited lung function. While many of these patients are treated with limited resection (wedge resection or segmentectomy), stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT) has emerged as an alternative treatment option. However, there is limited data regarding the effectiveness of SBRT. Methods: We used the Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results registry linked to Medicare claims to compare survival of patients treated with SBRT vs. limited resection. We identified all patients >65years of age with histologically confirmed NSCLC ≤5 cm in size and with negative (N0) lymph nodes. We compared baseline characteristics in each treatment group using the chi-squared test and t-test, as appropriate. We used logistic regression to create a propensity score model predicting SBRT use. We compared survival of patients treated with limited resection vs. SBRT after adjusting for propensity scores. Secondary analyses were performed stratifying the sample by type of limited resection (wedge vs. segmentectomy), age (≤75 vs. >75 years), and tumor size (≥3cm vs. <3cm). Results: We identified 2,243 eligible patients of which 362 (16%) received SBRT. Patients treated with SBRT were older, more likely to be unmarried, to have a higher comorbidity score and smaller tumors (p <0.001 for all comparisons). Adjusted analyses showed no significant differences in survival (hazard ratio [HR]: 1.18; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.97-1.45) among patients treated with SBRT vs. limited resection. While survival of patients who underwent SBRT vs. wedge resection was not significantly different (HR: 1.17; 95% CI: 0.95-1.44), SBRT was associated with worse outcomes when compared to segmentectomy (HR: 1.34; 95% CI: 1.02-1.75). When analysis was restricted to patients ≤75 yrs (HR: 1.08; 95% CI: 0.76-1.53) or >75 yrs (HR: 1.22; 95% CI: 0.96-1.55), and size ≥3cm (HR: 1.20; 95% CI: 0.96-1.50) or <3cm (HR: 1.11; 95% CI: 0.72-1.71) survival following SBRT and limited resection were not significantly different. Conclusion: SBRT is associated with similar overall survival when compared to wedge resection but not to segmentectomy in elderly patients with NSCLC ≤5cm and no lymph node involvement. Our results suggest that SBRT may be considered as an alternative therapy to limited resection in older patients who are not candidates for lobectomy.
