N2  - RATIONALE: Visceral pleural invasion (VPI) has been recognized as a negative prognostic factor in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). According to the most recent American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) Tumor, Node, and Metastasis (TNM) system, VPI leads to upstaging of cancers <3 cm but not of those 3-7 cm in size. However, previous studies have reached mixed conclusions as to whether VPI is an independent prognostic factor in early-stage lung cancer and whether its effect is size-dependent. METHODS: We used nationally-representative population-based data from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End-Results cancer registries. We identified 16,315 patients diagnosed with T1-T2 (i.e., <7 cm), N0, M0 NSCLC between 2004 and 2010 who underwent lobectomy. We compared lung cancer-specific (primary outcome) and overall (secondary outcome) survival in patients with and without VPI using the Kaplan-Meier method. A revised staging classification was derived based on the impact of VPI according to tumor size (<2 cm, 2-3 cm, 3-5 cm, and 5-7 cm). We used Cox regression analysis to assess the impact of VPI on survival after controlling for potential confounders; the c-statistic was used to compare the discrimination of the current AJCC staging system vs. the revised classification. RESULTS: Overall, 3,389 (21%) NSCLC patients had VPI. Unadjusted analyses showed that VPI was associated with significantly worse lung cancer-specific (p<0.0001) and overall (p<0.0001) survival. For each tumor size stratum, survival of patients with VPI more closely tracked that of patients without VPI in the immediately-larger size category (Figure 1). Adjusted analysis showed VPI to be an independent negative prognostic factor for lung cancer-specific (hazard ratio [HR]: 1.37, 95% CI: 1.28-1.46) and overall (HR: 1.23, 95% CI: 1.15-1.30) survival after controlling for potential confounders. A revised system was developed combining size and VPI, in which tumors with VPI were upstaged to the next T status. This revised system had improved discrimination compared with the current AJCC classification (c-statistic: 0.78 and 0.76, respectively). CONCLUSIONS: VPI is a negative prognostic factor in early-stage NSCLC, independent of tumor size. AJCC guidelines may be revised to better incorporate the impact of VPI on survival of patients with early stage NSCLC. Patients with VPI should be considered candidates for more aggressive management. (Figure Presented).
