N2  - Rationale The primary objective of this study was to define clinical baseline parameters that can predict survival of patients with stage I or II non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) who underwent surgery or stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT). Methods From January 2001 to January 2011, 425 patients (216 surgery, 209 SBRT) were identified with clinical stage I or II NSCLC. Cumulative survival was analyzed using the Kaplan-Meier method. The Cox proportional hazards regression analyses were used to investigate potential risk factors for mortality caused by tumor and patient specific factors. Results The median age of patients in the surgery and SBRT groups was 65 (range 39-83 year) and 74 (range 51-91) years, respectively. The median follow-up time for the whole group was 2.6 years (range 1-10 years). The types of surgical procedures performed were: wedge resection in 3% (n=4) of patients, lobectomy in 76% (n=163), bilobectomy in 7% (n=16), and pneumonectomy in 14% (n=30). The SBRT group consisted of patients with peripheral tumors. A larger proportion of the surgical group had a Charlson Comorbidity Index score of ≤1 compared to the SBRT group, 48% and 29%, respectively. In the surgery group 65% (n=140) of patients had stage I, 29% (n=62) stage II. In the SBRT group 88% (n=183) of patients had stage I, 12% (n=26) stage II. Median clinical tumor diameter in the surgery and SBRT group was 26 mm (range 5-136) and 30 mm (range 10-100), respectively. SBRT patients were older, had higher Charlson Comorbidity Index score, and lower forced expiratory volume in 1 second. Overall survival of the surgery group was 71% at 3 years and 60% at 5 years. For SBRT group this was 55% at 3 years and 34% at 5 years. We identified that older age, Charlson Comorbidity Index score 4, and clinical stage IIB were associated with higher long-term mortality in patients undergoing surgery. In the SBRT group we identified that Charlson Comorbidity Index score ≥5, and clinical stage >IA were associated with higher long-term mortality. Conclusions Both patient characteristics and survival of NSCLC I and II patients undergoing surgical treatment or SBRT differ considerably. Long-term survival of lung cancer patients can be determined by focusing on patient and tumor specific factors. In addition to TNM the consideration of patient age and Charlson Comorbidity Index may improve prognostication of patients with NSCLC, and assist in selecting an appropriate treatment strategy.
