N2  - BACKGROUND: Surgical resection remains the first-line treatment for stage I NSCLC. Therapeutic options are limited for patients with severe lung disease who are deemed medically inoperable. SBRT is a technique utilizing highly conformal, ablative doses of radiation. Phase II trials have shown that SBRT is effective in achieving local control but is associated with toxicity when used to treat central tumors, presumably from injury to proximal airways.1,2 CASE REPORT: 62yo male former heavy smoker with severe COPD on supplemental oxygen was found to have a right upper lobe (RUL) apical segment tumor <1cm in size while undergoing bronchoscopic evaluation for tracheobronchomalacia (TBM) [Image A]. Biopsy revealed invasive squamous cell carcinoma. The tumor could not be visualized on CT or PET/CT. Clinical staging was assessed as T1N0M0. Due to his poor lung function, he was not a candidate for a right upper lobectomy. He opted to undergo SBRT rather than endobronchial brachytherapy. Two coiled metal intralesional fiducials (Visicoil™) were placed bronchoscopically in order to locate the tumor radiographically and guide therapy [Image B]. He received 50 Gy in five fractions over fifteen days and tolerated the therapy well without complications. He was followed bronchoscopically post-SBRT. Initially at 3 weeks, there was a small amount of residual abnormal mucosa in the RUL apical segment [Image C]. Endobronchial biopsy revealed atypical squamous metaplasia. At 15 weeks, scar tissue was present at the takeoff of the RUL apical segment, but there was otherwise no evidence of residual endobronchial tumor [Image D]. After achieving successful local control of his tumor, his respiratory status remains stable. DISCUSSION: This unique case illustrates successful treatment of purely endobronchial T1N0M0 NSCLC with SBRT in a patient with TBM. At four months of follow up, the patient's respiratory status remained stable despite having received high-dose radiation to his proximal bronchial tree. SBRT delivery and subsequent bronchoscopic surveillance were extremely challenging due to the tumor's small size. However, bronchoscopic placement of fiducials guided therapy without causing significant artifact. Using a protocol of 50 Gy in five fractions allowed for safe treatment of this small central tumor, with no noted toxicity at four months of follow up. As our three week bronchoscopic surveillance showed radiation effect, we suggest performing this study 12-16 weeks after completing therapy. (Figure Presented).
