N2  - Rationale: Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) is a treatment option for stage I non-small cell lung cancer in patients who are medically inoperable and/or who refuse to have surgery. According to the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) Guidelines (2013), “SBRT has achieved primary tumor control rates and overall survival, comparable to lobectomy and higher than 3D-CRT in nonrandomized and population-based comparisons in medically inoperable or older patients.” SBRT can be achieved by using several methods: respiratory gating, body frame, or target tracking (fiducial markers or fiducial free markers). We report our center's SBRT cone beam CT and orthogonal XR for respiratory gating without fiducial markers and our SBRT toxicity outcomes. Methods: We report back only 9 months of data, annualized to a year-by-year average. January marked our institutions completion of a huge treatment machine upgrade; operating at 60-70% treatment capacity. Comparing 2011 and 2013, there has been a growth of over 20% in the numbers of patients receiving SBRT for lung cancer, due to changes in practice compared to earlier years. From January to September 2013, we have treated 35 patients with Lung SBRT. Two patients from this number received 2 courses of lung SBRT each (to separate lung lesions, not diagnosed at the same time). However annualized, this totals to 46.5 patients (average) per year. Results: To date, the 35 patients (2013) receiving SBRT for lung cancer have not re-presented with excessive early pulmonary toxicity. Conclusion: Fiducials in the lung is a topic that is being explored throughout Radiation Oncology practice. Radiation Oncologists are trying to reach a consensus, whether select patients might benefit from this, with the addition of triggered imaging and arc therapy. 3The necessity of FMs, given the frail patient population, need for general anesthetic, cost, 10% miss rate, and small pneumothorax causes the potential of unnecessary risk. The importance of these risks is magnified because stereotactic treatments are being delivered routinely without FMs on linear accelerators with outstanding oncologic outcomes and very favorable toxicity. Our physicians would agree that the fiducial techniques (even should they be supported in future publications) would not be appropriate for all patients, or all SBRT lung patients. Our center has excellent local tumor control rates with low toxicity with SBRT without the use of fiducial markers. However, fiducial applications in the lung, are under consideration for wider use in the future. (Figure Presented).
