N2  - Purpose/Objective(s): The reports that the escalation of radiation dose > 60 Gy would not benefit further the patients with unresectable stage III non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) draw attention to the surgical role in combination with concurrent chemoradiation (CCRT). The current study was conducted to look into the outcomes of surgical salvation after curative high-dose CCRT. Materials/Methods: Between 2004 and 2013, twenty stage III NSCLC patients underwent surgical salvation after curative intent full-dose CCRT ≥ 60 Gy (Group 1). Median age was 65 years. Initial stage was IIB in 2 patients (10%), IIIA in 10 (50%), and IIIB in 8 (40%). Radiation dose was 60-90 Gy (median, 66 Gy). As the comparative group (Group 2), we got the data of 12 patients with planned induction CCRT and surgery in the same period. Median age was 63 years. Initial stage was IIB in 1 (8.3%) and IIIA in 11 patients (91.7%). Radiation dose was 50-55 Gy (median 55Gy). All of 32 patients received the combination of platinum-based paclitaxel, docetaxel, or gemcitabine doublet chemotherapy concurrently with RT. Median time from completion of CCRT to surgery was 49 days. Twenty-three lobectomies and 9 pneumonectomies (1 right, 8 left) were performed. Median follow-up was 2.8 years. Results: Of all 32 patients, twenty-five patients (78%) indicated no residual lymphadenopathy, and eight (25%) exhibited a complete pathologic response. Primary tumor was down-staged in 94% (30/32) patients and lymph node was down-staged in 72% (23/32). There was no significant difference of response rates between groups. Surgical mortality was 25% (5/20) in Group 1 and 8.3% (1/12) in Group2 (p = 0.248). Chronic empyema was shown in 13.3% (2/15) in Group 1 and 0% in Group 2. In Group 1, there was no local failure and 3 patients died with distant failure. In Group 2, one patient (8.3%) experience the local failure at the follow-up time of 15 months after surgery and one patient showed the distant failure. Twelve patients (80%) in Group 1 and nine patients (82%) in Group 2 were still alive at the time of analysis and their median survival was not yet reached in both groups (p = 0.917). All of seven patients with ypT0N0 were still alive without disease recurrence. Median survival was 29 months in patients with ypN+, in a while median survival was not yet reached in ypN0 (p = 0.097). We compared the various clinical and dosimetric parameters between patients with or without surgical mortality. The only statistically significant parameters affecting to the surgical mortality were V5Lung (42% vs 30%, p = 0.026) and V10Lung (33% vs 25%, p = 0.033). Conclusions: The surgical salvation after curative dose chemoradiation might benefit in carefully selected patients. The risk of operative mortality was expected to be increased in patients with higher percentage of V5 or V10 of the lung. This experience will require confirmation in a prospective multi-institutional clinical trial.
