N2  - Stereotactic ablative radiotherapy (SABR) has become an established modality for the curative treatment of early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) in patients who are unfit for surgery. Matured SABR data have confirmed the early reports of high local control with low rates of severe toxicity, while preserving quality of life and pulmonary function. These outcomes have prompted the question whether SABR can also be regarded as an alternative to surgery in operable patients. Onishi et al. described the results of SABR in 87 Stage I NSCLC patients who were medically operable but refused surgery in a multi-institutional setting, reporting a 5-year local control and overall survival rate of 87% and 70%, respectively. A subsequent Amsterdam study reported similar findings in 177 operable patients with local control and survival rates of 93% and 85% at 3-year follow-up. In view of the observation that SABR cohorts generally consist of elderly patients with considerable co-morbidity, these results appear to be similar to those of historical surgical series. However, as only randomized trials can answer the question whether equivalence exists between surgery and SABR, several prospective studies have been initiated in recent years. A lack of patients recruitment, which appears to be prominently due to doctors treatment preference, has led to premature closure of all initiated trials. Therefore, only historical comparisons and data such as from matched pair analyses can be used to determine equipoise between both treatment modalities. A recently published propensity score-matched pair analysis [Verstegen 2013] comparing SABR and video-assisted thoracosopic surgery showed similar overall survival and a slightly better local control for the SABR cohort, although only 64 patients were available in each arm. In order to make an appropriate choice between SABR or surgery for any patient, several parameters need to be considered, and discussion within a multi-disciplinary tumor board meeting is essential. Age and comorbidity are major determinants, because in a recent Dutch national review [unpublished data] the 30-day mortality after surgery for stage I NSCLC increased from 0.6% in patients less than 60 years of age to 3.8% in patients over 80 years of age. Patients need to be optimally staged, including at least FDG-PET-CT scans, and preferably in operable patients some form of minimallyinvasive mediastinal staging through mediastinoscopy, EUS and/or EBUS [Silvestri 2013; de Leyn 2013]. A number of local and international trials evaluation these minimally-invasive staging procedures as a routine procedure in combination with SABR for operable patients are being initiated, among others in the Netherlands. Finally, it is important to realize that salvage surgery remains an option in cases where an isolated local recurrence is diagnosed following SABR. A number of small recent studies have shown that salvage lobectomy is safe and feasible if performed in a timely manner, even in patients initially considered to be medically inoperable.
