N2  - Introduction: SABR was introduced as standard treatment for medically inoperable stage 1 NSCLC in Nottingham in 2012. We present details of the first 20 pts treated up to November 2013. Methods: Referrals from local lung cancer MDTs. Inclusion criteria from SABR Consortium guidelines: Biopsy proven NSCLC (or PET positive growing lesion) <5 cm, T1/T2 N0M0, refused or declining surgery. PS≤3, pt able to cooperate with and tolerate 4DCTand maintain treatment position for 40 minutes. No min FEV/TLCO was stipulated. Patients were planned and prescribed as per local protocol (defined from SABR consortium). The patients were imaged pre treatment with cone beam 4DCT, soft tissue matching performed and then rescanned to confirm set up accuracy before each fraction. Daily post treatment CT for audit of immobilisation and accuracy. Results: All 20 pts completed full planning incl 4D CT, 19 pts have completed all fractions. (Table presented) The majority of the pts were referred due to cardiac co-morbidity (8) but 3 pts had had previous lobectomy/pneumonectomy. Analysis of the post treatment moves in the first 10 pts revealed a mean maximal movement of 0.19 cm (0.1 0.3) well within the 5mm margin applied to PTV. Conclusion: SABR is safe, well tolerated and implemented within a standard clinical lung cancer radiotherapy practice. All 20 pts so far completed planning and treatment as prescribed. Follow up so far only months but fatigue is only Gd 3 toxicity seen.
