N2  - Rationale: Limited resection is commonly used for treating stage I-II non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients who cannot tolerate lobectomy. However, these patients are at increased risk of local recurrence. The potential role of postoperative radiotherapy (PORT) following limited resection is not well established. Methods: We used national data from the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) registry linked to Medicare claims to identify 3,052 patients with stage I-II NSCLC (≤5 cm in size) that underwent limited resection (wedge resections or segmentectomy) between 1992 and 2007. We used logistic regression to determine propensity scores for patients receiving PORT based on the patient's postsurgical characteristics. Then, we compared survival of patients treated with and without PORT using a Cox regression adjusting for propensity scores. We also used instrumental variable (IV) analysis to obtain a consistent estimate of the effectiveness of PORT even in the presence of unmeasured confounders. We used Health Care Service Areas (HCSA) as our IV; we classified patients as residing in a high-utilization or low-utilization area based on the proportion of patients in the HCSA that received PORT. The IV was calculated as the difference between the adjusted survival between the high- and low-utilization areas, divided by the probability of undergoing RT among NSCLC patients in those regions. The confidence interval (CI) of the IV estimate was obtained using bootstrap. Results: Overall, 359 (12%) patients received PORT within 3 months of limited resection. PORT treated patients were younger (p=0.01), more likely to be males (p=0.03), and unmarried (p=0.02); there were no significant differences in estimated income or race/ethnicity. Patients with T2 tumors (p<0.0001) or N1 disease (p<0.0001) were also more likely to receive PORT. Adjuvant chemotherapy was also more frequently used among PORT treated patients (p<0.0001). Cox survival models adjusted for propensity scores showed that PORT was associated with increased mortality in patients with stage I-II cancers (hazard ration [HR]: 1.43; 95% CI: 1.24-1.65). Similar results were obtained in analyses stratifying by cancer stage. IV analyses also showed increased mortality with PORT at 1 year (IV estimate for absolute difference in survival -5%; 95% CI: -1% to -12%) and 2 years (-12%; 95% CI: -2% to -27%) after surgery. Conclusions: PORT is not associated with improved survival among elderly patients who underwent limited resection for early stage NSCLC. Other strategies are needed to improve outcome of patients who are not candidate for lobectomy.
