N2  - Dyspnea is a common complaint encountered by general practitioners and subspecialists. When an individual with a prior pulmonary malignancy presents with dyspnea, often there is a fear of recurrent cancer. Paraneoplastic syndromes (PNS) are potential causes of dyspnea. Commonly associated with small cell lung cancers (SCLC), PNS generally improve with treatment of the underlying malignancy. Hence PNS are less often considered in the non-small cell cancer (NSCLC) and post-therapy settings, leading to delay in recognition. A 52-year-old man presented with a 7-month history of intermittent dyspnea. His medical history was significant for 50-pack-years of tobacco use, stage I adenocarcinoma surgically resected, moderate COPD, and coronary artery disease. One month after his lobectomy he experienced abrupt onset of dyspnea, described as a feeling of restriction of both his breathing and ability to phonate. This was associated with an upper abdominal pressure-like discomfort, exacerbated by cool temperatures, and with evidence of left trepopnea. Locally, a PET scan was negative for residual or new malignancy. Additionally, coronary angiography and echocardiography were unrevealing. Our initial testing included pulmonary function testing, which found a mixed pattern with mild restriction and FEV1 of 43%, decreased maximal inspiratory pressure, and decreased diffusing capacity. Chest CT scan showed no evidence for recurrent cancer or pulmonary embolism. Direct laryngoscopy found normal vocal cord function. While there was no evidence of diaphragm paralysis, chest fluoroscopy revealed reduction of downward diaphragmatic excursion with inspiration and sniff. A diaphragm electromyography recorded incomplete bilateral phrenic neuropathies with active denervation and reinnervation during testing. Due to his history of lung cancer, neuroimmunological testing was pursued revealing collapsin response mediator protein 5 IgG (CRMP-5 IgG) and N-type calcium channel antibody seropositivity. The neuromuscular ganglion was unaffected, which made Lambert-Eaton syndrome less likely. His symptoms were diagnosed as a CRMP-5-associated PNS. The patient was treated with a five week course of intravenous methylprednisolone. On follow-up evaluation, he noted marked improvement in his dyspnea and functional status. PNS associated with pulmonary malignancies are not rare to many pulmonology practices. CRMP-5, a neural specific antibody, is associated with SCLC and thymoma. Clinical effects include peripheral and autonomic neuropathy, encephalopathy, and myelopathy. CRMP-5 IgG is detected by Western blot analysis and can be seen in the presence of other autoantibodies. This case is unique in that the neoplasm associated with phrenic neuropathy was a NSCLC. Prompt recognition of PNS may reduce morbidity in patients with lung cancer.
