N2  - Objectives: Our unit is enthusiastic about video-assisted thoracoscopic (VATS) lobectomy and believe there are few contraindications to attempting the VATS approach for lung cancer resection. We compared clinical outcomes of VATS resection between early and advanced lung cancer to explore the suitability of VATS for Stages II and III disease. Methods: Prospective data was collected for 200 patients (87 [43.5%] VATS and 113 [56.5%] thoracotomy) who underwent lobectomy for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) between October 2008 and October 2012. We compared the outcome for VATS for early (Stage I, n = 68) vs advanced (Stage II, III, n = 19) lung cancers. Results: Median age was similar in both groups early vs advanced 67.5 [48-85] vs 71 [51-85] years (P = 0.52). Mean preoperative FEV1 was early 85 ± 18% vs 80 ± 25% (P = 0.41). There were no significant differences between groups for median postoperative drain duration 4 (2-42) vs 3 (2-22) days (P = 0.75) and median length of stay 6 (3-56) vs 5 (3-69) days (P = 0.66). One early stage patient had an in situ carcinoma at bronchial resection margin. In-hospital mortality was 1 (1.5%) for early tumours and 2 (10.5%) for advanced tumours (P = 0.056). Conclusions: We believe that it is possible to achieve comparable early postoperative outcomes for Stage II and III NSCLC as with Stage I with VATS. With increasing experience the contraindications for a VATS approach are getting fewer and fewer.
