N2  - Introduction: The determination of emphysema severity in patients with lung cancer undergoing surgical resection is currently under active investigation. We assessed the 5-year survival of patients with biopsy proven Stage I NSCLC stratified by a visual analog scale for the severity of emphysema. Methods: CT scans of 153 consecutive patients with biopsy-proven stage 1 NSLC diagnosed between 2001-2006 were assigned an emphysema severity score in four regions of the lung. All CT files were read by an independent radiologist and an epidemiologist blinded to the patient records for agreement. The lungs were divided into four regions: The Right upper quadrant (RUQ), Right lower quadrant (RLQ), Left Upper quadrant (LUQ) and left lower quadrant (LLQ). The middle lobe was considered part of the upper quadrant. For each region, the degree of emphysema was assessed in all four quadrants separately. The scale used was: 0 = no emphysema, 1 = less than 25% emphysema, 2 = 25 to 50% emphysema, 3= 50-75% emphysema, and 4= more than 75% emphysema. Scores from the four quadrants were added up for a total score for the patient. 5-year survival follow up was completed on all patients. Results: Group 1: 25.5% (39/153) of patients had no emphysema, Group 2: 60% (92/153) had a total emphysema score of <= 7, and Group 3: 14.5% (22/153) a score of more than 7. 88.9% of patients underwent lobectomies (136 out of 153), and 11.1% underwent wedge resection (17/153). The distribution of operation was balanced for the emphysema groups (p=0.391). Furthermore, the three groups were comparable for Age, gender, asbestos exposure, and location of lung cancer (p = 0.78, p= 0.589, p=0.678, p= 0.302) respectively. However, the groups were significantly different for Smoking status, total pack years, FEV-1% of predicted, and FEV/FVC % of predicted (p<0.001, p<0.001, p=0.04, p = 0.001) respectively. Using the Log Rank (mantel-cox) test, survival was significantly different at 5 years. (p=0.037) Figure attached. Conclusions: The degree of emphysema highly influenced 5 year survival for patients with biopsy proven Stage I NSCLC. A visual analog scale for the determination of the degree of emphysema was easy to use and highly discriminatory of survival. (Figure Presented).
