N2  - Introduction: Chemotherapy and radiotherapy are the mainstay for the treatment of small cell lung cancer (SCLC) as the disease is usually advanced at presentation. Some reports suggest surgery may play a role in selected cases with early stage disease, although cases are usually accumulated over a long period with heterogeneous treatment. We report herein our 5-year experience with anatomical lung resection for SCLC as part of multimodality treatment. Methods: Retrospective review of all patients operated on for SCLC between January 2007 and December 2011. Results: Fifteen cases of SCLC were identified, (2 mixed adenocarcinoma/SCLC, 1 mixed squamous cell/SCLC). Median age was 68 years (range 55 to 84) and 7 patients were female. 1330 resections for NSCLC were performed in the same period of time in this unit. The histological diagnosis was established preoperatively as SCLC in 3 patients, as NSCLC in 4; 3 of which turned out to be mixed tumours and was not established in the remaining 8 patients. Pathological tumour stage (TNM 7th edition) was I in 8 cases; II in 6 cases; and III in 1 case. There was no operative mortality and one prolonged air leak. One patient had received induction chemotherapy prior to surgery. Eleven patients received adjuvant chemotherapy; 3 patients were assessed as unfit for chemotherapy. Additionally 3 patients received adjuvant thoracic radiotherapy and 5 received prophylactic whole brain irradiation. To date 3 patients have relapsed (7, 20 and 34 months) all of whom are dead. There were no other deaths. One-year and 2-year survival were 100% and 75% respectively. The median follow up of the 12 survivors was 19 months (range 10 57 months) without relapse. Conclusion: These contemporary data suggest anatomical resection as part of multimodality therapy for early stage SCLC provides excellent local control and reasonable survival at an acceptable risk. (Figure Presented).
