N2  - Objectives: The impact of the massive early lung cancer detection programme on patient profile and surgical activity of the local thoracic surgery department is analysed. Methods: Early lung cancer detection programme was run in a single city of 400 000 inhabitants from 2008 to 2011. Enrolment criteria included both sexes aged 55 - 65 years with 20 pack-years of tobacco smoking. All detected lesions were followed up in accordance with IELCAP protocols including referrals to the local thoracic surgery serving for the population of approximately 2 million people (10% of them are aged 55 - 65). Following data were analysed: number of NSCLC detected and resected, number of stage I resected patients, histology, type of surgery. Results: A total of 15 020 patients were screened (7.5% of the population of people aged 55 - 65 in our region, 0.75% of the entire population). Of these, 6240 pulmonary lesions were detected, majority (59%) smaller than 5 mm. Four hundred and fourty-five lesions (7.1%) were bigger than 15 mm. Hundred and eighty-two patients (2.9% of all detected lesions) were referred to the local thoracic surgery. Between 2008 and 2011, 925 NSCLC were resected at our department. Of these 232 (25%) were stage I. The number of pneumonectomies was 166 (18%). Dominant histology was squamous cell (52%) 247 metastatic lesions were resected in the same period. The early detection group delivered 77 NSCLC resections (8.3% of all resections). Of these, 53 (69%) were stage I (significant difference versus the entire group). The number of pneumonectomies was 2/77 (2.5%, significant difference). Dominant histology was adenoca (50%). Thirty-three metastatic lesions (13.3% of all metastases resections) were treated surgically. Fifteen small cell lung cancers were diagnosed. Conclusions: The patients with NSCLC detected by the early lung cancer detection programme are treated surgically in earlier stages in comparison to the entire population of patients referred to our department. They require pneumonectomy less frequently than the entire group.
