N2  - Rationale: The purpose of this study was to compare positron emission tomography with computed tomography (PET/CT) against non-contrast chest CT alone in monitoring for recurrence 1-year status post lobectomy of stage 1 non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). For surveillance after treatment with curative intent, current (October 2011) National Comprehensive Cancer network (NCCN) guidelines recommend chest CT with or without contrast every 6-12 months for 2 years, then non-contrast chest CT annually. (category 2B recommendation) PET/CT is not currently indicated for routine follow-up. Methods: Ninety patients who received a surveillance PET/CT 1 year after lobectomy for the treatment of stage 1 (including 1a and 1b) NSCLC were included in the study. Exclusion criteria included the presence or interval diagnosis of a second malignancy, or surgical treatment more radical than single lobectomy. The non-contrast CT obtained at the time of the 1-year PET/CT was interpreted by an experienced chest radiologist, blinded to the PET/CT findings and clinical information, for evidence of recurrence using the following data points: pulmonary nodule, pleural effusion, pleural mass, adenopathy, and extrathoracic mass. The decision about recurrence was made solely from the non-contrast CT and without PET/CT findings. This was then compared with the determination made from the PET/CT. The reference standard for determination of recurrence was the clinical decision made by the multi-disciplinary tumor board who had access to all imaging and clinical data. Results: Recurrence at 1 year was documented in 16 of the 90 patients (17.8%). Of the 16 recurrences, all were documented with PET/CT, and only 9 were found with non-contrast CT alone (56.3%). Five of the 7 recurrences missed with non-contrast CT alone were extrathoracic metastases (71%). Three recurrences reported on CT and 5 recurrences reported on PET/CT were documented as false positive by the tumor board. Sensitivity and specificity of CT for recurrence was 56.3% and 95.9%, respectively. Sensitivity and specificity of PET/CT for recurrence was 100% and 93.2%. Sensitivity of CT is shown to be statistically significantly different from that of PET/CT with p = 0.015 using McNemar's chi-squared test. There is no statistically significant difference for specificity between PET/CT and CT with p = 0.62. Conclusions: PET/CT is more sensitive than non-contrast CT alone in monitoring for recurrence of stage 1 lung cancer one-year status post lobectomy.
