N2  - Rationale: Complete resection of stage 1 and 2 non-small cell lung cancer(NSCLC) offers the best chance for cure. 5 year survival rates are between 30 to 60%1. There have been no factors specifically attributed to early disease recurrence. A study of patients undergoing curative surgery for NSCLC, from aDGH, to assess recurrence rate and associations between referral source, lung function, time to surgery from diagnosis and upstaging at surgery. Methods: Retrospective case note review of patients managed with curative resection (lobectomy and pneumonectomy)for NSCLC between 2009 and 2010. The Wilcoxon test was used for statistical analysis. Results: 42 patients in total, with a mean age of 72 (range: 53 to 89) and a male:female ratio of 29:13. Early recurrence occured in 9(21.4%). 2 died soon after surgery due to unrelated causes. results are shown in table. No patients with recurrence had per-operative upstaging, compared to 5 in the other group. Conclusion: We conclude from our study that the presence of symptoms at time of referral, a lower pre-operative FEV1 and histological confirmation of adenocarcinoma, were associated with early recurrence, in our practice. This would suggest the need for a larger study to look into causes of early recurrence following curative surgery for lung cancer. (Table Presented).
