N2  - In the non-surgical treatment of stages I-II NSCLC, a growing body of high-level evidence supports the routine use of SABR. Multi-center prospective trials have reported in-field tumor control rates exceeding 90% [Baumann P, 2009: Timmerman B, 2010]. Since 2007, SABR has increasingly been considered as a standard of care for a small peripheral lung tumor. The introduction of SABR in the province of North Holland has been shown in a population-based time trend study to correlate with a decrease in the proportion of patients aged 75-years and older who were untreated, an increase in the proportion treated with radiation, and improved survival in irradiated patients [Palma D, 2010]. An analysis of elderly patients with stage I NSCLC in the entire Dutch population between 2001-2009 (n = 4605 patients), revealed an increase in median survival from 16.8 months to 26.1 months in the radiotherapy group [Haasbeek CJ, Proc WCLC 2011]. Significantly, survival in untreated patients (6.6 months) was unchanged and was similar to that reported in previous decades, and the 90-day post-surgical mortality was 7%. Such population-based data has established SABR as a preferred treatment for elderly patients, even in the absence of a randomized clinical trial comparing SABR with conventionally fractionated radiotherapy. Encouraging data is available on the preservation of quality of life post-SABR, limited loss of pulmonary function and in patterns of late toxicity. While a prospective RTOG trial in centrally-located tumors is ongoing, published data using a risk-adapted SABR scheme has reported good local control rates with use of 8 fractions of 7.5 Gy [Haasbeeek CJ, 2011]. Variations in the rates of local failure between centers, as well as in chest wall toxicity, highlight the importance of implementing a quality assurance program [Hurkmans C, 2009, 2010]. Although primary tumor recurrence rates typically range from 6-10%, nearly 25% of patients can develop late focal, mass-like, lung fibrosis post-SABR that can be difficult to distinguish from recurrence [Dahele M, 2011]. A systematic review has identified features on follow-up CTand PET-scans that are suspicious for a local tumor recurrence [Hwang K, in press Radiotherapy and Oncology]. Reports on outcomes of SABR in potentially operable patients are now available [Onishi H, 2011; Lagerwaard FJ, 2011]. In addition, outcomes of a propensity score-matched analysis in stages I-II NSCLC treated using either lobectomy by video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) or SABR were also favorable for SABR [Senan S, submitted]. In summary, tangible progress has been made using SABR in early-stage NSCLC in patients who are unfit or at high-risk for complications. Radiation oncologists have every reason to participate with enthusiasm in the ongoing ACOSOG Z4099 trial, where patients considered 'borderline operable' due to co-morbidity, are prospectively randomized to either primary surgery or SABR. In countries where no trials are ongoing, efforts to empower patients with a stage I NSCLC to participate in shared decision making should be encouraged. Shared decision making (SDM) is an active process in which physicians and patients work together to select a treatment, based on clinical evidence as well as the patient's preferences (http://www.informedmedicaldecisions.org).
