N2  - Introduction: VATS lobectomy for NSCLC provides safe, oncologically sound surgery with reduced immunological compromise compared with open lobectomy. Adjuvant chemotherapy is recommended for patients with adequate performance status (PS) following resection of NSCLC of TNM stage IB and above. We compared the delivery and toxicity of chemotherapy in patients following open and VATS lobectomy. Method: Retrospective study of all patients who had resection of primary NSCLC in a single surgical centre between October 2008 and April 2011. Surgical and chemotherapy databases were reviewed to extract data on patient characteristics, operative details, pathological stage, chemotherapy delivery and toxicity. Statistical comparisons were made by standard parametric, non-parametric, and contingency tests, with P <0.05 indicating statistical significance. Results: There were 125 NSCLC cases identified. 57 (45.6%) underwent VATS resection and 68 (54.4%) patients had thoracotomy; 14/57 (24.5%) and 16/68 (23.5%) of each group respectively had adjuvant chemotherapy. Patient demographics and tumour stage were similar between VATS and thoracotomy groups; median age: 67.8 (53.0-83.0) vs. 69.0 (50.2-75.6) (P = 0.50); sex: 50% vs. 50% male (P = 1.0); stage I/II: 71.4% vs. 60% (P = 0.70); stage III: 21.4% vs. 40% (P = 0.43); pre-chemotherapy PS of 0: 50% vs. 68.8% (P = 0.46). All patients received platinum/vinorelbine therapy. Chemotherapy was initiated significantly earlier in the VATS group (median 53 days (30-82) vs. 67 days (35-83), P = 0.05). Although dose intensity was similar between VATS and thoracotomy groups (% planned platinum dose: 68.4% vs. 72.8%; % planned vinorelbine dose: 69.2% vs. 71.5%; P = 0.66 and P = 0.84 respectively) there was a trend to reduced grade 3/4 haematological toxicity in the VATS group (30% vs. 60%, P = 0.15). Conclusions: VATS lobectomy facilitates earlier delivery of adjuvant chemotherapy in patients with resected NSCLC and may also be associated with reduced toxicity. This study provides evidence for an additional benefit for VATS lobectomy in appropriate NSCLC patients.
