N2  - Objective: Although video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) has been widely adopted for early stage primary non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), its oncologic outcome is still debatable. To clarify this issue, we designed a retrospective study by setting up comparable groups for each surgical approach and compared the oncologic outcomes. Methods: Between March 2005 and June 2010, a total of 1147 patients underwent lobectomy for NSCLC. Among them, VATS lobectomy was attempted in 326 patients, and conventional thoracotomy was performed in 821. There were 7 postoperative deaths, 2 in VATS group (0.6%) and 5 in thoracotomy group (0.6%) and those were excluded from the study. We stratified each group based on patients' age, sex, smoking history, and clinical stage. Comparable patient groups were then constructed by selecting suitable patients from each group by matching stratified clinical variables. Using this method, 321 patients were selected on each group and were used for final analysis. Statistical analyses were conducted using the student t-test, chi-square test, and the survival analyses were performed using Kaplan- Meier's method with log-rank test and Cox's proportional hazards model. Results: Among 321 patients of the VATS lobectomy group, 27 patients (8.4%) were converted to thoracotomy. The 3-year overall survival was 85.4% in VATS group, and 88.6% in thoracotomy group and there was no statistical differences (p = 0.565). The 3-year disease-free survival (DFS) was 83.2 % and 77.1%, in each group and showed no difference (p = 0.584). Age (p = 0.000), size of the tumor (p = 0.030), sex (p = 0.008), and pathologic N stage (p = 0.000) were associated with worse survival in multivariate analysis, whereas the method of surgery was not ( p = 0.406). For DFS, age (p = 0.008), size of the tumor (p = 0.000), and pathologic N stage (p = 0.002) were statistically significant factors and, again, the method of surgery was not analyzed for significant factor (p = 0.756). Conclusions: In our study, we demonstrated the overall survival and DFS was similar between VATS lobectomy and conventional thoracotomy groups when their clinical variables had been matched the same. Our results suggested that in an early stage NSCLC, VATS lobectomy could provide the same quality of oncologic outcomes in our current clinical judgment of selecting patients for VATS procedure.
