N2  - Introduction: Long-term cure rates of patients (pts) with early and locally advanced non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC) have improved in the past decade but remain suboptimal. Patterns of treatment failure in E and LA NSCLC are as follows: (a) local and loco-regional relapse (b) distant relapse outside the brain (c) brain relapse (d) treatment-related events (e) co-morbidity-related events, and (f) second cancers. Any effort to improve survival must take into account these competing risks. Additionally, important factors for improving pts outcome include (1) the availability/experience of modern diagnostic tools and treatments and (2) collaboration within multidisciplinary team. Material and Methods: The current literature on treatment strategies in stages I to IIIB NSCLC including randomised controlled clinical trials and meta-analysis were reviewed. Results: the treatment of NSCLC can vary considerably due to comorbidities, age and a large heterogeneity within each stage of NSCLC. The new IASLC/UICC staging classification is the cornerstone for treatment decisions based on pt prognosis. Stage IIIA (N2) is a very heterogeneous stage. The “Robinson classification” (5 categories: IIIA(0)1 to IIIA4) was proposed for this purpose, but may not be the optimal tool for further refinement of this stage. Standard and accepted treatments: stage I: surgery (S) if fit (+/ adjuvant chemotherapy (CTx) in larger IB tumour); alternative/unfit for S: stereotactic RTx>(S-RTx); if unfit for S and/or larger tumors: RTx alone or CTx/RTx; Stage II: fit for S: S + adjuvant CTx; unfit for S: definitive RTx or CTx/RTx; unfit for CTx (eg.renal failure/dialysis): local Tx alone (S alone or S-RTx or RTx alone). Stage III: only found at S: S + adjuvant CTx; fit for S: induction Tx (CTx or CTx/RTx) + S; unfit for S: definitive CTx/RTx; unfit for CTx (eg. renal failure/dialysis): local Tx (RTx alone/S alone). For most pts with stage IIIA4 and IIIB disease, definitive CTx/RTx is the treatment of choice. Important exceptions: T4N0-1, Pancoast tumors, where CTx/RTx + S standard of care. Age <75 80 and co-morbidities (FEV1,DLCO, EF) are relevant selection factors for aggressive approaches. Most relevant data stage II: IALT, ANITA, BLT, ALPI, NCIC, LACE. Most relevant data stage III: RTOG, INT0139, CTx/RTx Meta-analysis JCO 2010, LACE. However, large and mature/randomized data sets on newer techniques such as VATS lobectomies, sublobar resections, stereotactic radiotherapy and others, are not available yet.
