N2  - The final aim of surgical treatment for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is complete resection for which specific criteria have been defined [1]. The 7th edition of the TNM classification for lung and pleural tumours was introduced in 2009 [2]. When considering surgical treatment for NSCLC, a distinction is made between early stage disease (stages IA/B and IIA/B), locally advanced disease (stages IIIA/B), and metastatic disease (stage IV). Indications for surgical treatment can be subdivided into definite, investigational and exceptional (table 1). Although no prospective, randomized trials exist comparing surgery and radiotherapy in early stage NSCLC, surgical treatment has traditionally been considered the treatment of choice in early stage and T3N1 disease due to its good long-term results. Surgery for locally advanced disease remains controversial although phase II and III studies have shown that surgical resection is feasible after induction therapy. Surgical resection for stage IIIA-N2 is currently only indicated in patients with mediastinal downstaging after induction therapy who can preferentially be treated by lobectomy to obtain a complete resection. In specific cases of T4 disease long-term survival may be obtained after surgical resection, sometimes in combination with chemo- or radiotherapy. Most important prognostic factors include complete surgical resection, excellent performance status and no involvement of mediastinal nodes. In certain subgroups of T4N0 disease a 5-year survival of 30% has been reported. This specifically relates to tumours invading the main carina or distal trachea, left atrium, superior vena cava or in case of superior sulcus tumours (including Pancoast tumours) and ipsilateral, malignant nodules in the non-primary lobe. Table presented.
