N2  - Neuroendocrine tumors constitute a distinct tumor entity disclosing morphologic and biologic neuroendocrine features. They are classified in 4 histopathological types and 3 malignancy grades. The typical carcinoids are of low grade (G1), the atypical carcinoids of intermediate grade (G2), the large neuroendocrine carcinoma and the small cell carcinoma are high grade neuroendocrine tumors (G3). Their distinction relies on objective morphologic and phenotypic criteria of strong clinical significance and predictive prognostic value (Brambilla 2005). Pulmonary carcinoid tumors are rare malignant neoplasms, accounting for 2-5% of all lung tumors. Carcinoid tumors typical or atypical are solid tumors, symptoms and physical findings depend on their location. Peripheral tumors are often asymptomatic, cough, haemoptysis and recurrent infection constitute the classic triad of symptoms by central tumors. Most tumors can be located and identified. by CT-scan and fibre bronchoscopy Nodal involvement and histological subtype appear as the most important factors influencing prognosis. (Garcia-Yuste 2007). Adequate lung resection and systematic lymphadenectomy should always be performed. Sleeve resection is safe and effective in central typical and atypical carcinoid tumors, avoiding pneumonectomy. 5-years survival rates are 90-98% for typical and 60-88% for atypical carcinoid tumor (Bini 2008, GarciaYusta 2007). Among all NSCLC, large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma (LCNEC) is the tumor with the worst prognosis. Nodal invasion clearly decreases the possibility of long- term survival in these patients, confirming the importance of preoperative and perioperative staging. (Garcia-Yuste 2008) Tumor recurrences occur in the form of intrapulmonary metastasis and distant metastasis (Jungraitmayr 2006). Surgery alone is not sufficient so that multimodality therapies should be evaluated in large multiinstitutional trials. Iyoda (2006) reported about 2 years survival rate of 88.9% in 15 patients with NCNEC after surgery and adjuvant treatment with cisplatin and VP-16. Veronesi (2006) reported about 5-year survival of 52% for stage I, 59% for stage II and 20% of stage III and suggested that chemotherapy may improve prognosis also in stage I disease.
