N2  - Background: The aim of this study is to describe current management strategies across Europe and their impact on clinical outcomes and overall resources burden. Methods: The EPICLIN-Lung study (NCT00831909) is a non-interventional prospective cohort study conducted in Belgium, France, Germany, Greece, Italy, Portugal, Spain and Turkey. Patients with confirmed NSCLC attending a participating clinical department for the first time between January and March 2009 were included. Minimum follow-up was 1 year or until death (maximum 15 months). Descriptive analyses were performed. Results: In total, 3,508 patients were recruited with a mean (±SD) age of 64.5 (±10.5) years. 77.6% of patients were male; 37.6% presented with locally advanced (Stage II-III) and 48.6% with metastatic (Stage IV) disease. In all, 22.7% of patients underwent first surgical procedure (mainly lobectomy [57.8% of procedures]); 65.5% and 34.5% surgical procedures were performed on patients with non-advanced (Stage I) and advanced/metastatic disease (Stage II-IV), respectively. The mean (±SD) length of hospitalisation due to the first surgical procedure was 11.4 (±8.0) days. Radiotherapy (mainly palliative [52.5% of treatments]) was indicated in 1,225 patients (34.9%). First chemotherapy regimen was usually combination therapy (78.0%) with cisplatin plus gemcitabine, carboplatin plus gemcitabine and cisplatin plus vinorelbine used in 13.5%, 11.5% and 10.0% of patients, respectively. Percentages varied across countries (from 29.9%, 6.9% and 24.1% in Belgium to 1.3%, 3.8% and 3.2% in Greece, respectively). Mean (±SD) length of first-line treatment regimen was 71.1 (±51.2) days after first dose. Cisplatin plus vinorelbine was the most common regimen in patients with non-advanced disease (19.0% of patients), cisplatin plus gemcitabine was the most common regimen in patients with locally advanced/metastatic disease (13.9% of patients). Updated study data will be presented at the congress. Conclusion: This study provides a clinically important description of the management patterns of NSCLC across Europe and highlights differences between individual countries at all stages, from initial surgery to subsequent therapies.
