N2  - Purpose: To compare computed tomography (GT) and fluorodeoxyglucosepositron emission tomography (FDG-PET) for delineation of gross tumor volume (GTV) in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and to validate results with the macroscopic surgical specimen. Materials: Nine patients with stage 1-11/ NSCLC treated by lobectomy were prospectively enrolled. Before surgery, all patients underwent gated contrastenhanced CT and FDG-PET. Next, the lobectomy specimen was removed, inflated with gelatine, frozen (-80 0 C for 48h) and sliced. The digitized slices were used to reconstruct the 3D macroscopic specimen. GTVs were manually delineated on CT, using lung (GTVCTLWI and mediastinal (GTVCTMW) windows, and on the macroscopic surgical specimen (GTVmacro). The GTVs were automatically segmented on PET images using a gradient-based method relying on watershed and clustering (GTVPetW&C), a source to background ratio method (GTVPetSBR), and fixed threshold values, I.e. 40% (GTVPet40%) and 50% SUV max (GTVPet50%). All images were registered. The volumetric analyses included the absolute volume assessment and the ratio between GTVs defined on the imaging mooalitles and the macros copy. A matching analysis with Dice's similarity index (DSI) was performed between the GTVmacro and those defined on CT and FOG-PET. Results: On average, the GTVCTLW and GTVCTMW significantly overestimated the GTVmacro (46.6 ml, 41.1 ml versus 12 ml, respectively), whereas the PET-based GTVs were smaller (GTVPetW&C 13.5 ml, GTVPetSBR 13 ml, GTVPet40% 13.3 ml and GTVPet50% 8.1 ml). Among the various methods for PET segmentation, the gradient-based method gave the best estimate of the macroscopic volume on a case-by-case basis when looking at the ratio analysis. Interesti ngly, for patients with no atelectasis or lung fibrosis, the mediastinal windowed CT accurately assessed the true tumor volume. Last, the matching analysis did not reveal significant difference between the various imaging modalities, the DSI ranging from 0.57 to 0.67. Conclusions: FDG-PET greatly improved the GTV definition in NSCLC patients when the primary tumor was surrounded by modifications of the lung parenchyma. In this context, the gradient-based method outperformed the threshold-based methods in terms of accuracy and robustness. In other cases, the conventional mediastinal windowed CT remains appropriated.
