N2  - Objectives: To determine if positron emission tomography (PET) scanning has improved short and long-term survival of patients undergoing potentially curative resection for lung cancer. PET scanning is thought to be very accurate in assessing nodal and systemic metastasis, and thus the prevention of non-curative resections. No publication has demonstrated an increased survival in lung cancer due to the use of PET scanning. Methods: A prospective thoracic surgery database was retrospectively analysed. Patients who had undergone potentially curative resections for nonsmall cell lung cancer, n=1883, who had a PET scan preoperatively, n=665, were compared to patients who had not undergone PET scanning, n=1218. PET scanning became routine for all patients four years ago in our unit. Staging was defined as pathological staging to eliminate bias by 'better' preoperative staging due to PET scanning. Routine intraoperative mediastinal lymph node sampling was undertaken. Results: PET scanning did not improve short or long-term survival in patients undergoing resections for stage I (PET n=439, no PET n=852), P=0.46, II (PET n=145, no PET n=273), P=0.37 or IIIa (PET n=9, no PET n=17), P=0.69 nonsmall cell lung cancer. PET has, however, resulted in a significant increased survival for patients undergoing wedge resections for primary lung cancer (PET n=72, no PET n=76), P=0.03. Conclusions: Patients undergoing wedge resection for non-small cell lung cancer should have PET scans performed. For patients with stage I, II, and IIIa non-small cell lung cancer, the role of PET scanning needs to be further evaluated.
