N2  - Purpose: To assess the acute toxicity and efficacy outcomes of Iodine-125 (125I) mesh brachytherapy after wedge resection in medically high risk stage I non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients. Materials and Methods: Between June 2006 and October 2009, patients with poor performance status or respiratory reserve that were deemed unfit for lobectomy (surgeons discretion) for stage I NSCLC were considered for wedge resection by open or video-assisted thorascopic surgery plus intraoperative mesh brachytherapy with 125I. All patients received 100 Gy directed at the staple line plus a 5-mm margin using a single plane technique. Here, we review the acute toxicity and efficacy seen in these patients based on a retrospective review of our prospectively maintained surgical database. Results: A total of 28 patients were identified that underwent mesh brachytherapy. Twenty-six patients had T1N0 tumors and 2 patients had T2N0 disease. The average tumor size was 2.1 cm (range 0.7 to 5.5 cm). All margins were negative on frozen section with the average closest margin of 11.5mm (range 1mm to 37 mm). With a median followup of 7.3 months (range 1 month to 37 months), there were 0 local recurrences. Two patients had distant recurrences. There were no failures in the same lobe of the wedge resection. The mean survival and mean disease free survival were 35.5 and 33.8 months, respectively (median survivals not reached). The overall survival and disease-free survival at 38 months was 85.7% and 84.6%, respectively. No radiation related toxicities were observed. Three patients had air-leaks that resolved and only 1 patient had a postoperative infection that also resolved. No late toxicities (>3 months after surgery) were seen. Conclusions: Though our results are early, it appears that 125I mesh brachytherapy is a reasonable option for patients with stage I NSCLC that cannot undergo lobectomy. The high local control suggests this may be reasonable option for patients with medically operable disease and prospective studies are currently under development.
