N2  - Percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) for thoracic malignancies debuted in 2000 and is considered a safe, effective and minimally invasive technique for non-surgical management of pulmonary malignancies. Whilst surgery remains the mainstay of treatment in early stage non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), the occurrence of new malignancy in the contralateral lung of patients who have had previous pneumonectomy usually precludes further surgery. Some have also considered pulmonary RFA a contraindication in this subgroup of patients. We describe our experience with radiofrequency ablation of new primary malignancies in two single-lung patients. To our knowledge, there has only been one such case report in the literature.
