N2  - Background: Few patients diagnosed with lung cancer are still alive five years after diagnosis. However, overall 5-year survival rates of 30 50% following surgical resection for non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) can be expected. The aim of the current study is to conduct a 10-year review of a consecutive series of patients undergoing curative-intent surgical resection at the largest tertiary referral centre in the Republic of Ireland in order to identify prognostic factors. Methods: Case records of all patients operated on for lung cancer between 1998 and 2008 were reviewed. The clinical features and outcomes of those with surgico-pathological confirmed (p)-stages I to IIIA NSCLC were recorded. Results: Of a total of 650 patients who underwent surgical resection with curative intent during the study period, there were 559 with confirmed pathological stage I to IIIA disease (stage IA: 21.3%; stage IB: 36.3%; stage IIA: 4.3%; stage IIB: 19%; stage IIIA: 19.1%). Median overall survival for the entire cohort was 40 months. The median age at operation was 66 years, with males accounting for 61.7% of the total. Distribution of histological subtypes was: squamous cell, 50.6%; adenocarcinoma, 43.6%; and the large cell neuroendocrine variant and mixed cellularity carcinomas 7.9%. Lobectomies were performed in 82.3% of cases, while pneumonectomy was necessary in 17.7% of patients. The proportion of patients undergoing pneumonectomy decreased significantly in the latter half of the study (23% vs. 14.3%). Clinico-pathological characteristics associated with improved survival by univariate analysis includes: younger age, female sex, smaller tumour size, history of smoking, lobectomy, lower T and N status and less advanced pathological stage. Age, gender, smoking status and tumour size emerged as independent prognostic factors by multivariate analysis. Conclusion: We identified several factors that predicted improved outcome for NSCLC patients undergoing curative-intent surgical resection in Ireland. Survival rates in our series are comparable to those reported from other European thoracic surgery centres.
