N2  - Surgery offers the best chance for cure for patients with stage I or II non-smallcell lung cancer (NSCLC). However, with advances in operative techniques and improvement of perioperative management, more aggressive surgical resection can be attempted, for instance, even in patients with stage IIIB. About one-third of NSCLC presents with locally advanced stage III disease, and about half of them had stage IIIB. Although patients with stage IIIB disease are typically treated with definitive chemoradiation, surgery can be offered to highly selected patients as a single modality or after induction chemotherapy and/or radiotherapy. Patients who are staged T4 due to satellite tumour nodules, carinal involvement or superior vena cava invasion can be a good candidate for surgical treatment as long as there is no evidence of N2 or N3 disease. On the other hand, as opposed to aggressive approach for locally advanced disease, video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) lobectomy has been performed with increasing frequency for early-stage NSCLC as a minimally invasive approach. Advocates of VATS lobectomy emphasize the benefit in terms of less tissue trauma, reduced cytokine release, decreased postoperative pain, lower complication rates, and shorter hospital stay. A recent meta-analysis of randomized and nonrandomized trials on safety and efficacy of VATS lobectomy demonstrated that VATS lobectomy is an appropriate procedure for selected patients with early-stage NSCLC when compared with open surgery. However, questions still remain to be answered as to whether VATS lobectomy reduces the local recurrence and improves the long-term survival in patients with NSCLC. Therefore, more evidence supporting the oncologic efficacy of VATS lobectomy for patients with NSCLC needs to be accumulated to gain its widespread acceptance in clinical practice. Nevertheless, many studies favouring VATS lobectomy in terms of oncologic efficacy are being reported in recent days. Therefore, VATS lobectomy can be considered as a feasible option for patients that are surgically resectable as long as there is no compromise of standard oncologic principles of thoracic surgery.
