N2  - Objectives: This study was designed to test the reliability of sentinel node identification using a new mannose receptor-binding agent Technetium-99 m neomannosyl human serum albumin (99mTc-MSA) in stage I non-small cell lung cancer. Methods: 99mTc-MSA was administered at the peri-tumoral region under chest CT or bronchoscopic guidance 3 h before surgery. SPECT lymphoscintigraphic images were obtained at 30 min and 1 h after injection. The radioactivity of the lymph nodes was counted with a handheld gamma probe before (in vivo) and after (ex vivo) dissection. Lymph nodes with an ex vivo radioactive count >5 times the radioactivity count of the resected lung tissue were identified as sentinel nodes. Results: Forty-two patients (30 men, 12 women; mean age, 63.3±8.91 years) who were candidates for lobectomy with mediastinal lymph node dissection for stage I non-small cell lung cancer were enrolled consecutively. Sentinel nodes could be detected from 30 min to 5 h after the injection of 99mTc-MSA on lymphoscintigraphy. The number of dissected lymph nodes per patient was 22.1±11.55 (4-57). Sentinel nodes could be identified in 40 patients (95.2%). The number of sentinel nodes identified was 2.3±1.07 stations (range, 1-5) per patient. Ten of the 40 patients (25.0%) had metastases in 11 sentinel nodes. Three of these 11 sentinel nodes (27.3%) had skip metastases. No false-negative sentinel nodes were detected in any of the 10 patients with N1 or N2 disease (0%). Conclusions: 99mTc-MSA has promising properties for sentinel node identification in non-small cell lung cancer.
