N2  - Objective: Resection of the left upper lobe with preservation of the lingula is anatomically equivalent to right upper lobectomy, which preserves the right middle lobe. To evaluate thoracoscopic apical trisegmentectomy (ATS) as an alternative to thoracoscopic left upper lobectomy (LUL) for resection of nonsmall-cell lung cancers (NSCLC) localized to the left upper lobe, this study compared mortality, complication rates, recurrence, and hospital length of stay for each of these techniques. Methods: Between 1998 and 2008, 314 patients underwent LUL and 30 patients underwent ATS. The charts for these patients were reviewed retrospectively, noting pathologic stage, hospital length ofstay (LOS), occurrence and types of complications, cancer recurrence, and mortality. The Wilcoxon rank sum test was used to compare age, number of complications, and LOS between the 2 groups. The Fisher exact test was used to compare the complication rates between the two groups. The Kaplan-Meier method was used to estimate survival in each group and the log-rank test was used to compare survival between the two groups. Results: There was no statistical difference between the LUL and ATS group with respect to age, complications, cancer recurrence, and mortality. Sixty-three (20%) of the LUL group and 9 (30%) of the ATS group had a complication, P = 0.24. Twenty two (7%) of the LUL group and three (10%) of the ATS group had a recurrence of their cancer, P = 0.41. There were 61 deaths (19%) in the first 3.5 comparison years in the LUL group and 3 (10%) in the ATS group, P = 0.49. In addition, the ATS group had a statistically significant shorter LOS than the LUL group (median 2 vs. 3 days, respectively; P = 0.025. Conclusions: ATS for clinical stage I NSCLCs located in the apex ofthe left upper lobe, away from the junction of the upper division and the lingula, may represent a clinically acceptable alternative to standard LUL.
