N2  - Purpose: To establish characteristics of lung cancer patients diagnosed at the University Clinic of Respiratory and Allergic Diseases Golnik in 1996, their selected and realized therapy, and survival. Patients: 345 patients aged 37-90 years (mean 65), 285 male and 60 female. PS (Karnofsky) > 80 in 171 patients, 60-80 in 130 and < 60 in 44 patients. Microscopically confirmed tumor in 97%: by bronchoscopy 281, transthoracic needle biopsy 23, peripheral lymph nodes biopsy 12, sputum cytology 7, pleural (effusion) cytology 4, distant metastases biopsy 2, mediastinoscopy 1, autopsy 4 patients. Histology: squamous 131, adenocarcinoma 86, large cell 63, small cell 51, non-small cell 1, unclassified 2. Clinical staging of NSCLC: stage I 63, stage II 32, stage IIIA, stage IIIB 59, stage IV 77, undeterminable 2 patients. Staging in SCLC: LD 24, ED 27 patients. Results: Selected primary oncological therapy was changed in 11%. Realized primary therapy: radiotherapy 102 (30%), surgery 77 (23%), chemotherapy 47 (14%), supportive treatment 111 (33%). In resected patients staging was correct in 46%, underestimated in 44%, overestimated in 10%. Overall five-year survival was 7.8% (median 6.2 months) and five year survival of resected patients was 41.9 % (median 33 months). Median survival of irradiated patients was 5.7 months, of supportively treated patients 2.5 months. Survival was significantly different according to PS and stage. Conclusion: Selected oncological therapy was actually realized in 89%. In our patients there was a low percentage of NSCLC treated by chemotherapy. Among five-year survivors there were 26 resected and one supportively treated patient, that confirms surgery as most effective therapy in our lung cancer patients.
