N2  - If the diagnosis of SCLC is known, then complete thoracic and distant staging is mandatory before considering surgical resection. This staging must include imaging of the chest, brain, abdomen, and bone and mediastinoscopy, even if the results of mediastinoscopy are less reliable than for NSCLC. The current guidelines for the management of solitary pulmonary nodules, however, do not recommend obtaining preoperative tissue diagnosis. If such a nodule is found intraoperatively to be a SCLC, then intraoperative staging should be performed before making a decision to proceed with pulmonary resection, as is the case for NSCLC. Potential surgical candidates should be staged using the TNM staging system rather than the LD/ED staging system. When making decisions about the best treatment for SCLC, it should be borne in mind that a substantial percentage of patients have a higher pathologic stage than clinical stage. The precise role of surgery is difficult to ascertain in this disease because there is a dearth of solid, up-to-date data available. Comparing medical and surgical series is like comparing apples and oranges because the surgical series always have more highly selected patients. Nonetheless, the authors hope that many medical oncologists will agree with our recommendations. SCLC is considered to be a systemic disease from the outset by medical oncologists, whatever its stage at presentation. In contrast, surgeons often believe that there is a small subset of patients with small, peripheral stage IA disease and that these cases can be a relatively indolent form of disease that can be cured with surgical resection. The current recommendation is that these patients should be resected and receive four courses of chemotherapy. The evidence to substantiate this recommendation is weak, but it seems impossible to go against this popular wisdom. It is obvious that a prospective randomized trial would be highly desirable. The prognosis of completely resected stage I SCLC is similar to that of early-stage NSCLC. For stage IB disease, the recommendation for surgery and chemotherapy seems to be universal and accepted. What is less clear are the indications and results of induction therapy for this stage of disease. Stage II disease is a more of a problem, insofar as there is no reliable nonsurgical method at present to stage hilar lymph nodes. Clearly, the prognosis for stage II SCLC is less favorable, but in the absence of mediastinal lymph node metastases, resection followed by chemotherapy seems to be a reasonable option when stage II SCLC is found at the time of thoracotomy. These uncertainties underscore the difficulty in defining the precise role for neoadjuvant chemotherapy as opposed to adjuvant chemotherapy in stage II disease. The prognosis for stage III SCLC clearly does not justify surgical management outside of the setting of a prospective randomized trial. The problem is what to do when despite a negative mediastinoscopy stage III (ie, N2) disease is found at the time of thoracotomy. It is obvious that clinical judgment must be used. If the systematic nodal dissection shows that complete resection is feasible and that the nodal disease burden is minimal, that only a lobectomy is required and if the patient has excellent performance status and good pulmonary reserve, then it would be difficult to condemn the surgeon who proceeds with lobectomy following this systematic intraoperative assessment. On the other hand, to proceed with a pneumonectomy or a resection in a higher risk patient seems unjustifiable. Again, chemotherapy should be administered postoperatively. The only role for surgery in stage IV SCLC is for palliation of symptoms. There is a definite role for this type of surgery; for example, pleurodesis or pleuroperitoneal shunt placement and debridement and/or stenting of the central airways. There is no literature to support the administration of postoperative thoracic radiotherapy after a complete resection. As alluded to earlier, the authors believe that there is no justification for proceeding with an incomplete resection. Should the final pathology reveal positive margins, it would seem reasonable to proceed with chemotherapy and to keep radiotherapy in reserve for the management of a local recurrence. The role of PCI is outside the scope of this article.
